const BANK=[{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q1","lecture":1,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A 22-year-old patient abducts the arm during examination, moving it from the anatomical position directly away from the lateral trunk. Which anatomical term describes this movement?","options":["Adduction","Abduction","Flexion","Internal rotation"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Adduction brings a limb toward the midline.","Correct. Abduction moves the limb away from the midline in the frontal plane.","Incorrect. Flexion generally decreases the angle at a joint.","Incorrect. Internal rotation turns a limb around its longitudinal axis."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q2","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"During rehabilitation, a patient slowly lowers a dumbbell from a flexed-elbow position while the biceps remains electrically active and controls the descent. What form of contraction occurs in the biceps?","options":["Isometric","Concentric","Eccentric","Passive"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Isometric contraction has no appreciable change in muscle length.","Incorrect. Concentric contraction shortens the activated muscle.","Correct. Eccentric contraction lengthens the active muscle while resisting an external load.","Incorrect. Passive lengthening does not involve the described active biceps recruitment."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q3","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A 68-year-old man has progressively worsening calf pain after walking several blocks. Angiography demonstrates a chronic high-grade arterial stenosis with multiple small vessel-to-vessel connections distal to the obstruction. The foot remains warm at rest, and resting skin perfusion is adequate, but exercise provokes pain and delayed recovery. Repeat imaging shows that the connecting vessels are patent, without new thrombosis. Which hemodynamic limitation best accounts for adequate resting perfusion but insufficient oxygen delivery during increased muscle demand?","options":["The collateral vessels are anatomically patent but have insufficient flow reserve at higher demand.","Anastomoses prevent any perfusion distal to a stenosis.","The limb must lack any alternate arterial connections.","The distal circulation receives only lymphatic fluid during activity."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. connectivity does not guarantee sufficient conductance to meet exercise demand.","Incorrect. Anastomoses provide potential alternate routes rather than preventing distal delivery.","Incorrect. The stem specifically documents patent alternate connections.","Incorrect. Lymphatics return interstitial fluid and do not replace arterial oxygen supply."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q4","lecture":2,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A spinal cord specimen shows large multipolar somata whose axons leave the cord to innervate skeletal muscle. Which gray-matter region contains these lower motor neurons?","options":["Dorsal horn","Posterior funiculus","Anterior horn","Dorsal root ganglion"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Dorsal horn is predominantly sensory processing.","Incorrect. The posterior funiculus is white matter carrying ascending fibers.","Correct. Anterior horn contains somatic lower motor neuronal somata.","Incorrect. The dorsal root ganglion lies outside the cord and contains sensory somata."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q5","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A patient with an L4–L5 posterolateral disc herniation develops weakness consistent with compression of the traversing root rather than the exiting root. Which nerve root is most likely affected?","options":["L3","L4","S1","L5"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. L3 is not the typical traversing root at L4–L5.","Incorrect. L4 exits at this level rather than traversing past the disc.","Incorrect. S1 is typically compressed by L5–S1 disc herniation.","Correct. L5 traverses at L4–L5 and is classically affected by a posterolateral herniation."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q6","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"After descending-aortic surgery, a patient develops bilateral weakness and loss of pain/temperature below a thoracic level, with preserved vibration and joint-position sensation. Which anatomical-vascular pattern best explains this selective deficit?","options":["Bilateral posterior spinal territory infarction with loss of dorsal columns","Anterior spinal artery territory ischemia with relative dorsal-column sparing","Isolated injury to one dorsal root ganglion","Selective destruction of posterior funiculi with preserved motor pathways"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Posterior territory damage preferentially affects dorsal-column modalities.","Correct. The anterior spinal artery supplies much of the anterior and lateral cord, including motor and pain/temperature pathways, while dorsal columns may be spared.","Incorrect. A single DRG cannot cause bilateral motor and spinothalamic tract findings.","Incorrect. Posterior funiculus damage would disproportionately impair vibration and proprioception."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q7","lecture":3,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"The resting membrane potential of a neuron is substantially closer to the potassium equilibrium potential than to the sodium equilibrium potential. Which membrane property best explains this?","options":["Higher resting permeability to potassium","Higher resting permeability to sodium","Absence of potassium ions inside the cell","Complete closure of all resting ion channels"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Resting potassium leak conductance dominates the membrane potential.","Incorrect. Higher sodium permeability would move the resting voltage toward sodium equilibrium.","Incorrect. Potassium is concentrated intracellularly.","Incorrect. Leak channels remain active at rest."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q8","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"Extracellular potassium increases acutely while neuronal potassium permeability remains dominant. Which immediate change in the potassium equilibrium potential and resting voltage is expected?","options":["Both become more negative","The equilibrium potential becomes more negative but resting voltage is unchanged","Potassium equilibrium and resting potential become less negative","There is no effect until ATP runs out"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Raising extracellular potassium reduces its outward chemical gradient, rather than increasing negativity.","Incorrect. The predicted equilibrium shift is opposite to that stated.","Correct. Higher extracellular potassium makes E_K less negative, depolarizing rest.","Incorrect. The electrochemical shift occurs before ATP depletion."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q9","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A patient develops severe hyperkalemia and progressive weakness. Initially the membrane depolarizes, but the muscle fibers subsequently fail to generate normal action potentials despite ongoing electrical stimulation. What best explains the paradoxical failure of excitability?","options":["Potassium channels can no longer ever conduct","The sodium–potassium pump instantly reverses direction","Sodium equilibrium potential becomes exactly zero","Sustained depolarization inactivates voltage-gated sodium channels"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Potassium channels need not lose all conductance to cause depolarization block.","Incorrect. Pump reversal is not the central acute mechanism.","Incorrect. The sodium equilibrium potential does not become zero in this scenario.","Correct. Sustained depolarization maintains sodium channels in an unavailable inactivated state, preventing regenerative upstrokes."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q10","lecture":4,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Tendon histology reveals densely packed collagen bundles aligned with the principal direction of tensile loading. Which connective tissue category best describes the tendon?","options":["Loose areolar","Dense regular","Dense irregular","Reticular"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Loose areolar tissue has sparse fibers and abundant ground substance.","Correct. Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen bundles suited to unidirectional tension.","Incorrect. Dense irregular tissue has multidirectional fiber bundles, as in reticular dermis.","Incorrect. Reticular tissue supports organs with branching reticular fibers."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q11","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A patient with poor dietary intake has gingival bleeding and deficient wound healing. Fibroblasts form collagen polypeptides, but stable collagen production is impaired because an intracellular posttranslational modification does not occur efficiently. Which step is affected?","options":["Extracellular cleavage of procollagen terminal peptides","Desmosomal assembly","Hydroxylation of collagen proline and lysine residues","Conversion of type IV collagen into type I"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Terminal peptide cleavage takes place extracellularly.","Incorrect. Desmosomes are adhesion junctions, not the affected collagen step.","Correct. Vitamin C is required for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation necessary for stable collagen.","Incorrect. Collagen types are independently encoded and are not converted into one another."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q12","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A young man presents with recurrent hematuria and sensorineural hearing loss. His glomerular basement membrane has an abnormal sheet-forming collagen network, but ordinary dermal fibrillar bundles remain largely intact. Which collagen class is primarily implicated?","options":["Type IV","Type I","Type II","Type III"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Type IV forms a basement-membrane network and is associated with Alport syndrome.","Incorrect. Type I forms strong fibrils in tendon, bone, and dermis.","Incorrect. Type II predominates in cartilage fibrils.","Incorrect. Type III forms reticular fibers and supports many tissues."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q13","lecture":5,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"In a transverse peripheral nerve section, each fascicle is surrounded by a cellular connective tissue sheath that contributes to the blood–nerve barrier. What is this sheath called?","options":["Epineurium","Endoneurium","Myelin sheath","Perineurium"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Epineurium encloses the whole nerve.","Incorrect. Endoneurium surrounds individual axonal units.","Incorrect. Myelin is a glial membrane wrapping the axon.","Correct. Perineurium encloses each fascicle and contributes to barrier integrity."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q14","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A 29-year-old man sustains a deep forearm laceration. Nerve biopsy taken proximal to the lesion contains intact myelinated fibers, while a distal specimen obtained days later contains fragmented axons and myelin ovoids. A nearby nerve compressed by a splint shows patchy loss of compact myelin with most axons still intact. Which primary structural change would distinguish the traumatic lesion from the compression-associated conduction abnormality?","options":["The transected nerve has isolated demyelination and the compressed nerve has primary axonal loss.","The transected nerve exhibits Wallerian degeneration, whereas the compressed nerve has primary segmental demyelination.","Both necessarily have identical primary neuronal soma destruction.","The damaged myelin proves both nerves are undergoing primary CNS demyelination."],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Transection produces distal axonal degeneration; the descriptions are reversed.","Correct. distal axon breakdown after transection is Wallerian degeneration, distinct from primarily myelin-directed injury.","Incorrect. Axonal and myelin damage need not entail immediate death of the neuronal soma.","Incorrect. These are peripheral nerves, and myelin breakdown can accompany different mechanisms."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q15","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A researcher compares two equally sized peripheral axons. Both retain normal sodium gradients, but one has focal loss of compact myelin between two intact nodes of Ranvier. Although action potentials initiate normally at the proximal node, many fail to excite the next node when the demyelinated interval is long. Which electrical change across the damaged internode most directly accounts for conduction failure?","options":["Increased transmembrane current leakage and capacitance reduce the depolarizing current reaching the next node","Reduced membrane capacitance speeds passive spread but prevents channel recovery","Higher internodal resistance prevents longitudinal current from entering the next node","Increased axial resistance from loss of myelin reduces intracellular sodium concentration"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: insulation normally limits membrane leak and capacitance; demyelination reduces propagation safety.","Incorrect. Reduced capacitance would favor, not impair, rapid spread; demyelination increases effective capacitance.","Incorrect. Myelin increases membrane resistance; its loss lowers membrane resistance rather than creating a higher one.","Incorrect. Myelin loss principally changes membrane properties; a fall in intracellular sodium concentration is not the mechanism."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q16","lecture":6,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A student locates the sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies in the intermediolateral region of the spinal cord. Which segmental range supplies the typical sympathetic outflow?","options":["C1–C8","S2–S4","T1–L2","L4–S5"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Cervical segments do not host the standard sympathetic outflow column.","Incorrect. S2–S4 is sacral parasympathetic outflow.","Correct. Sympathetic preganglionic neurons occupy the thoracolumbar cord, classically T1–L2.","Incorrect. This range does not represent the thoracolumbar origin."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q17","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A preganglionic sympathetic axon enters the sympathetic trunk, passes through without synapsing, and reaches a ganglion near the abdominal aorta before establishing a synapse. Which pathway does it follow?","options":["Gray ramus communicans","Pelvic parasympathetic splanchnic nerve","Dorsal ramus","Abdominopelvic sympathetic splanchnic nerve"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Gray rami distribute postganglionic fibers back to spinal nerves.","Incorrect. Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic outflow from S2–S4.","Incorrect. Dorsal rami are mixed spinal nerve branches to the back.","Correct. Abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers through the chain to prevertebral ganglia."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q18","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A patient develops severe intestinal dysmotility after an inflammatory injury confined predominantly to neural elements between the circular and longitudinal muscular layers. A second patient has preserved propulsion but abnormal intestinal secretion following a lesion centered in the submucosa. Both have intact extrinsic parasympathetic input. Which pattern of selective intrinsic neural-network dysfunction best explains their contrasting findings?","options":["First Meissner, second Auerbach","Both lesions exclusively involve vagal nuclei","Both lesions selectively destroy dorsal root ganglia","First Auerbach myenteric, second Meissner submucosal"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. The locations and functions are reversed.","Incorrect. The lesions involve intrinsic enteric networks, not exclusively extrinsic vagal nuclei.","Incorrect. Dorsal root ganglia are not the described enteric ganglia.","Correct. Myenteric plexus coordinates motility; submucosal plexus regulates secretion and local flow."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q19","lecture":7,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A gymnast has a fatigue fracture of the narrow vertebral region between the superior and inferior articular processes, without vertebral translation. What structure is fractured?","options":["Pars interarticularis","Dens","Vertebral body centrum","Transverse foramen"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. The pars lies between the articular processes and can develop spondylolysis.","Incorrect. The dens is a feature of C2.","Incorrect. The vertebral body is the anterior weight-bearing mass.","Incorrect. Transverse foramina characterize cervical transverse processes."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q20","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A 47-year-old woman develops scapular weakness after two separate surgical procedures. Following a posterior cervical lymph-node biopsy, she has shoulder droop and marked difficulty shrugging against resistance but retains scapular retraction. After a later injury near the medial scapular border, retraction is impaired while shrug strength is preserved. Which pairing of injured nerves best explains the different patterns of motor loss?","options":["Thoracodorsal then radial","Spinal accessory then dorsal scapular","Dorsal scapular then spinal accessory","Axillary then musculocutaneous"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Thoracodorsal supplies latissimus and radial serves upper-limb extensors.","Correct. Spinal accessory innervates trapezius (shrug); dorsal scapular supplies rhomboids (retraction).","Incorrect. This reverses the observed deficits.","Incorrect. Axillary and musculocutaneous nerves do not explain the specific scapular patterns."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q21","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"Two patients sustain cervical spine injuries. The first undergoes forceful extension and develops abnormal anterior gapping between vertebral bodies; the second sustains a flexion injury with trauma involving the posterior margins of the vertebral bodies within the canal. Which pairing of vertebral longitudinal ligaments is most consistent with the distinct injury patterns?","options":["Posterior longitudinal then anterior longitudinal","Ligamentum flavum then supraspinous","Anterior longitudinal then posterior longitudinal","Interspinous then nuchal"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. The first ligament is anterior and restrains extension.","Incorrect. These posterior arch ligaments do not match the specified vertebral-body surfaces.","Correct. ALL is anterior and resists hyperextension; PLL lies posterior to vertebral bodies inside the canal.","Incorrect. Neither pairing matches the longitudinal vertebral-body ligaments."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q22","lecture":8,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A palmar skin specimen contains a clear, thin epidermal layer found between the granular and cornified layers, characteristic of thick skin. Which layer is this?","options":["Stratum basale","Stratum spinosum","Stratum granulosum","Stratum lucidum"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Basale is deepest and mitotically active.","Incorrect. Spinosum contains prominent desmosomal profiles.","Incorrect. Granulosum contains keratohyalin granules.","Correct. Lucidum is a clear layer most prominent in thick skin."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q23","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A 57-year-old patient develops fragile blisters and widespread erosions. Biopsy shows suprabasal epidermal separation: basal keratinocytes remain attached to the basement membrane, while their lateral attachments to overlying keratinocytes have failed. Which junctional protein complex is the principal site of failure?","options":["Integrin-rich basal hemidesmosomes anchoring to laminin","Desmoglein-containing desmosomes coupling neighboring keratinocytes","Claudin-rich tight junctions sealing extracellular pathways","Connexin-containing gap junctions transferring small molecules"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Hemidesmosomes bind basal cells to matrix; preservation of basal attachment argues against their primary loss.","Correct. Correct: desmosomal adhesion between keratinocytes is disrupted in acantholytic intraepidermal blistering.","Incorrect. Tight junctions regulate paracellular diffusion rather than being the major mechanical linkage between keratinocytes.","Incorrect. Gap junctions permit intercellular signaling, not the main tensile adhesion needed here."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q24","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"Two siblings undergo testing after markedly different pigmentation responses to summer sun exposure. Both exhibit keratinocyte p53 activation and increased POMC-derived signaling after ultraviolet exposure. One develops normal delayed tanning, while the other has lifelong hypopigmentation with normal melanocyte counts and little pigment transfer. Which downstream defect best explains failure to tan in the latter patient despite intact upstream signaling?","options":["Loss of epidermal Langerhans cell antigen presentation","Failure to increase dermal capillary flow following UV exposure","Impaired melanocyte melanin synthesis after MC1 receptor pathway stimulation","Defective hemidesmosomal coupling of basal cells to the lamina"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Immune antigen presentation does not directly account for preserved melanocytes failing to synthesize pigment.","Incorrect. Vasodilation can affect erythema but not the enzymatic production of melanin.","Correct. Correct: a melanin synthesis defect can prevent pigmentation even with normal upstream UV signaling.","Incorrect. Basal adhesion defects cause fragility/blistering rather than isolated impaired pigment biosynthesis."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q25","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A 54-year-old woman has painful oral erosions, fragile flaccid blisters, and a positive Nikolsky sign. Biopsy shows intraepidermal acantholysis with basal keratinocytes still attached to basement membrane. Which autoimmune mechanism best explains her disease?","options":["Autoantibodies to desmoglein-dependent keratinocyte adhesion","Autoantibodies to type IV collagen in glomerular basement membranes","IgA deposition primarily at dermal papillary tips","Autoantibodies to BP180 causing subepidermal separation"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Pemphigus vulgaris targets desmogleins, causing intraepidermal acantholysis and mucosal disease.","Incorrect. Type IV collagen basement membrane disease does not explain flaccid epidermal blisters.","Incorrect. Papillary IgA is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis.","Incorrect. BP180 targeting produces bullous pemphigoid and usually tense subepidermal bullae."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q26","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A 76-year-old man develops intensely itchy tense bullae. Histology reveals subepidermal separation and direct immunofluorescence shows linear IgG at the dermal–epidermal junction. Which adhesion apparatus is primarily attacked?","options":["Desmosomes of suprabasal keratinocytes","Keratinocyte gap junctions","Dermal elastin fibers","Hemidesmosomal basal epidermal attachment"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Desmosomal autoimmunity causes intraepidermal pemphigus, often flaccid.","Incorrect. Gap junctions are not the primary target of bullous pemphigoid.","Incorrect. Elastin damage does not typically produce linear junctional IgG bullae.","Correct. Hemidesmosomal targets including BP180/BP230 underlie subepidermal bullous pemphigoid."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q27","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A child has skin fragility from infancy, healing with scarring and contractures. Testing reveals a defect in type VII collagen associated with anchoring fibrils beneath the basement membrane. Which epidermolysis bullosa subtype is most consistent?","options":["Junctional EB","Dystrophic EB","EB simplex","Bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Junctional EB most often involves laminin 332 or related junctional proteins.","Correct. Dystrophic EB is associated with collagen VII anchoring fibril defects and scarring.","Incorrect. EB simplex commonly involves keratin 5/14 and an intraepidermal cleavage plane.","Incorrect. Bullous pemphigoid is acquired autoimmune blistering, classically in older adults."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q28","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A teenager with severe scarring nodulocystic acne is prescribed isotretinoin after inadequate response to other treatments. Which counseling issue requires the most stringent preventive measures before and during therapy?","options":["Temporary reduction in sweating","Possibility of comedone formation","High risk of severe fetal malformations following exposure during pregnancy","Potential for isolated superficial pigmentation of healed lesions"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Sweating changes are not the primary special safety requirement.","Incorrect. Isotretinoin is intended to improve severe acne and comedonal pathogenesis.","Correct. Isotretinoin is strongly teratogenic and requires pregnancy-prevention safeguards where applicable.","Incorrect. Post-inflammatory pigmentation can follow acne but is not the most critical drug-specific hazard."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q29","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A 24-year-old woman has recurrent axillary nodules and painful sterile inflammatory collections. Early lesions lack established tunnels, but several months later deeper communicating tracts appear. Serial follicular histology is available. Which earliest change most plausibly precedes the later abscess-like inflammation and tunnels?","options":["Primary eccrine duct obstruction without follicular involvement","Follicular plugging and subsequent follicular rupture releasing contents into surrounding tissue","Isolated apocrine gland hypertrophy before any keratinocyte change","Initial dermal collagen VII loss causing subepidermal cleavage"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Primary eccrine obstruction is not the initiating lesion of hidradenitis suppurativa.","Correct. Correct: follicular occlusion and rupture initiate inflammatory tissue injury that can progress to tunnels.","Incorrect. Apocrine hypertrophy is not the primary upstream driver of the described follicular disorder.","Incorrect. Collagen VII defects cause mechanobullous disease rather than inflammatory follicular tunnels."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q30","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"Two women describe increased scalp hair loss. One reports diffuse shedding beginning several months after hospitalization for a major illness, with no scalp inflammation. The other has slowly progressive widening of her central part over several years and a family history of similar thinning. Examination reveals nonscarring alopecia in both. Which difference in hair-follicle biology most directly explains the distinct patterns and time courses?","options":["The first has more follicles entering telogen after a stressor; the second has androgen-driven follicular miniaturization.","The first has irreversible follicular scarring; the second an acute bacterial folliculitis.","Both have identical autoimmune destruction of follicles.","The first has a deep fungal infection and the second has primary loss of melanocytes."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Telogen effluvium causes delayed diffuse shedding from shifted cycling, while androgenetic alopecia is progressive miniaturization.","Incorrect. The first is nonscarring and the second is not described as infectious.","Incorrect. The two patterns have different causes and neither is classically explained by the same autoimmune process.","Incorrect. Neither fungal invasion nor melanocyte loss fits these patterns."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q31","lecture":1,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A 19-year-old student falls onto an outstretched hand and undergoes wrist radiography. A fractured carpal bone is approximately cuboidal and lacks an elongated diaphysis or prominent epiphyses. How is this bone classified by shape?","options":["Long bone","Flat bone","Short bone","Irregular bone"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. long bones have a shaft and distinct ends.","Incorrect. flat bones have broad, flattened surfaces.","Correct. carpal bones are generally classified as short bones.","Incorrect. complex irregular shapes characterize vertebrae and certain skull bones."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q32","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoing shoulder rehabilitation holds her forearm stationary against a resistance device. Electromyography demonstrates sustained contraction of the relevant muscles, but the joint angle and muscle length remain essentially unchanged. Which mechanical form of contraction is occurring?","options":["Concentric isotonic contraction","Isometric contraction","Eccentric isotonic contraction","Passive elastic recoil"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. muscle shortens as it produces joint motion.","Correct. tension develops without a change in muscle length or joint angle.","Incorrect. activated muscle lengthens against resistance.","Incorrect. the stem documents sustained active electrical recruitment."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q33","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A patient performs two elbow-flexion rehabilitation exercises using the same handheld weight. With the forearm nearly vertical, the weight's line of action passes near the elbow axis; when the forearm is horizontal, the perpendicular distance increases. The biceps moment arm is unchanged. Which alteration in external mechanics accounts for the greater muscle force required in the horizontal position?","options":["Increasing the external resistance moment arm raises resistance torque; the same muscle moment arm therefore requires greater muscle force.","Increasing the resistance moment arm raises resistance torque, but the required muscle force remains unchanged because the weight is constant.","Increasing the resistance moment arm lowers the resistance torque because the limb is farther from its pivot.","The greater muscle force results from an increased gravitational acceleration acting on the same weight."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Torque is force multiplied by perpendicular moment arm. With muscle moment arm constant, increased external torque requires greater muscle force.","Incorrect. Constant weight does not imply constant torque; the force moment arm changed.","Incorrect. A larger perpendicular moment arm increases rather than decreases external torque.","Incorrect. Changing position does not change gravitational acceleration or the magnitude of the weight."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q34","lecture":2,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A student examines a sensory ganglion containing large neuronal somata surrounded by satellite cells. Each neuron extends a single process that divides into a peripheral branch receiving sensory information and a central branch entering the spinal cord. Which morphological neuronal class is represented?","options":["Bipolar","Multipolar","Anaxonic","Pseudounipolar"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. classically associated with special sensory pathways.","Incorrect. typically motor neurons and many interneurons.","Incorrect. lacks the described bifurcating peripheral/central process.","Correct. a single process bifurcates into central and peripheral limbs."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q35","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A 43-year-old man has a penetrating injury near the posterior vertebral column. He subsequently develops localized posterior trunk numbness and weakness of the deep intrinsic back muscles, but limb strength is largely preserved. Which branch of the mixed spinal nerve was most likely damaged?","options":["Dorsal ramus","Dorsal root","Ventral root","Ventral ramus"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. mixed branch supplying intrinsic back and nearby posterior skin.","Incorrect. a sensory root carries no somatic motor fibers.","Incorrect. motor-only root, not a mixed cutaneous branch.","Incorrect. distributes primarily to anterolateral trunk and limbs."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q36","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A woman with fever, meningismus, and photophobia undergoes lumbar puncture at L4–L5. After the needle traverses the ligamentum flavum, it enters the epidural space containing fat and the vertebral venous plexus. Which meningeal layers must it next penetrate to reach cerebrospinal fluid surrounding the cauda equina?","options":["Pia mater followed by arachnoid mater","Dura mater then arachnoid mater","Arachnoid mater then dura mater","Dura mater then pia mater"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. pia adheres to cord/roots and is not penetrated to sample CSF.","Correct. both layers separate epidural space from subarachnoid CSF.","Incorrect. reverses their superficial-to-deep order.","Incorrect. omits arachnoid and incorrectly penetrates pia."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q37","lecture":3,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"In a neuron at rest, the membrane voltage lies substantially closer to the potassium equilibrium potential than the sodium equilibrium potential. The sodium–potassium pump maintains both gradients, but the resting potential is determined largely by relative passive ionic conductances. Which membrane property explains the predominance of the potassium equilibrium potential?","options":["2 Na+ out, 3 K+ in","3 Na+ in, 2 K+ out","3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in","2 Na+ in, 2 K+ out"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. reverses actual stoichiometry.","Incorrect. reverses transport direction.","Correct. ATP-driven exchange maintains electrochemical gradients.","Incorrect. neither correct stoichiometry nor direction."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q38","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"In a paired-stimulus experiment, a neuron cannot produce a second action potential immediately after the first despite intense stimulation. A little later, a larger-than-normal stimulus succeeds while the membrane is still recovering. Which sequence of voltage-gated sodium-channel states accounts for the initially complete lack of responsiveness followed by its gradual recovery?","options":["Na+ channel inactivation persists at first; channel recovery plus residual K+ conductance permits only stronger stimuli later.","Na+ channels remain continuously open while K+ channels disappear.","Potassium channels become irreversibly blocked, preventing repolarization.","Loss of the Na+/K+ pump restores action-potential threshold."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Na+ channel availability recovers while membrane excitability remains reduced.","Incorrect. persistent Na+ opening would not explain the initial refractory state.","Incorrect. delayed recovery does not require irreversible K+ blockade.","Incorrect. pump failure does not explain millisecond refractoriness."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q39","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A man develops perioral paresthesias and progressive paralysis after eating improperly prepared pufferfish. Serum electrolytes and neuronal resting membrane potentials remain relatively preserved, but recordings show failure of the rapid action-potential upstroke. The Na+/K+ ATPase is functional. Which membrane conductance is blocked by the toxin?","options":["Selective inhibition of delayed rectifier potassium-channel efflux","Block of voltage-gated sodium-channel inward current","Increased sodium-channel inactivation from severe extracellular hyperkalemia","Inhibition of the electrogenic sodium-potassium ATPase"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Potassium efflux primarily mediates repolarization, while a relatively preserved resting potential and absent fast upstroke point elsewhere.","Correct. Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated sodium channels and thereby prevents the regenerative sodium upstroke.","Incorrect. This can produce depolarization block, but serum potassium and the resting potential are relatively preserved.","Incorrect. Pump inhibition disrupts longer-term gradients and contradicts preserved pump activity."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q40","lecture":4,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A tendon specimen shows tightly packed extracellular fibers running in parallel with the direction of muscle pull. During contraction, the tendon transmits force to bone with little stretching. Which mechanical property is provided primarily by the collagen-rich fiber bundles?","options":["Resistance to tensile pulling","Rapid elastic recoil","Active contraction","Generation of synovial fluid"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. aligned collagen bundles resist longitudinal tension.","Incorrect. this is chiefly conferred by elastic fibers.","Incorrect. extracellular collagen does not contract.","Incorrect. not a function of tendon collagen."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q41","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Several days after surgical repair of a deep skin laceration, a biopsy demonstrates cells with abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum actively depositing extracellular matrix. The inflammatory infiltrate has diminished and tissue rebuilding is underway. Which connective tissue cells account for this collagen production?","options":["Mature adipocyte","Mast cell","Active fibroblast","Erythrocyte"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. primarily lipid storage and signaling.","Incorrect. releases inflammatory mediators.","Correct. abundant rough ER supports collagen and ECM protein synthesis.","Incorrect. lacks organelles and collagen biosynthesis."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q42","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Two cartilage samples contain similar fibrillar collagen networks, but one has markedly depleted negatively charged glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans. During repeated compressive loading, the altered tissue deforms more and recovers poorly despite preserved collagen fibers. Which property of the extracellular matrix is chiefly responsible for the difference?","options":["Increased actin-myosin force generation","Reduced fixed negative charge and osmotic water retention","Increased abundance of elastin crosslinks","Loss of neuronal voltage-gated channels"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. cartilage ECM is not contractile.","Correct. loss of anionic GAGs diminishes hydrated compressive support.","Incorrect. not the stated molecular loss.","Incorrect. unrelated to cartilage compressive behavior."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q43","lecture":5,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A section of spinal cord shows large multipolar neurons with prominent basophilic Nissl substance. A cone-shaped zone near each axon origin remains pale and lacks the rough endoplasmic reticulum responsible for Nissl staining. Which neuronal region produces the pale appearance?","options":["Dendritic spine","Perikaryon","Satellite cell","Axon hillock"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. dendrites can contain Nissl substance proximally.","Incorrect. cell-body rough ER forms conspicuous Nissl substance.","Incorrect. glial support cell, not the neuron's cone-shaped origin.","Correct. lacks rough ER/Nissl and connects the soma to initial segment."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q44","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A patient is exposed to a compound that interferes with termination of signaling at the skeletal neuromuscular junction. Ordinarily, released acetylcholine briefly activates receptors in the motor end plate before its effects end. Which enzyme normally breaks down acetylcholine within the synaptic cleft?","options":["Acetylcholinesterase","Monoamine oxidase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Choline acetyltransferase"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. hydrolyzes acetylcholine to terminate receptor activation.","Incorrect. acts on monoamines, not the main NMJ transmitter.","Incorrect. catecholamine synthetic enzyme.","Incorrect. synthesizes ACh, does not terminate its action."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q45","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A severed peripheral nerve is surgically reapproximated. In one patient, distal axons degenerate but Schwann cells remain organized in longitudinal pathways, and partial function returns over subsequent months. In a second patient, destruction of those pathways impedes useful regrowth. Which Schwann-cell-associated structure guides regenerating axons?","options":["Regenerating axons follow aligned Schwann-cell bands in the distal nerve stump.","Regrowing axons follow oligodendrocyte processes that remyelinate the distal stump.","Regenerating axons grow along newly formed Nissl bodies inside the distal nerve.","Regrowing axons use nodes of Ranvier as a continuous structural scaffold across the gap."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Schwann-cell Bands of Büngner provide longitudinal guidance for regenerating peripheral axons.","Incorrect. Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS fibers; peripheral nerve guidance is provided by Schwann cells.","Incorrect. Nissl substance is in neuronal somata and dendrites rather than serving as a distal axonal track.","Incorrect. Nodes are sites of saltatory excitation, not a continuous repair-guiding structure."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q46","lecture":6,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A physiology student compares motor pathways supplying skeletal muscle with those supplying smooth muscle and glands. Somatic motor axons usually travel directly from the CNS to skeletal muscle, whereas autonomic pathways synapse in a peripheral ganglion. How many neurons typically form the efferent autonomic pathway to the target organ?","options":["One neuron","Three neurons","Two neurons","Four neurons"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. typical of somatic motor innervation.","Incorrect. not the usual autonomic efferent arrangement.","Correct. preganglionic and postganglionic neurons.","Incorrect. does not describe the classic ANS efferent chain."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q47","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"During thoracic surgery, a branch linking a spinal nerve to the sympathetic trunk is identified. It carries myelinated preganglionic sympathetic axons from the spinal nerve into the chain and is present only at thoracolumbar levels. Which communicating structure is this?","options":["Gray ramus communicans","Pelvic splanchnic nerve","Dorsal root ganglion","White ramus communicans"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. carries postganglionic fibers out of the trunk.","Incorrect. conveys sacral parasympathetic fibers.","Incorrect. contains sensory neuronal cell bodies.","Correct. carries myelinated preganglionic sympathetic fibers into the chain."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q48","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A man with exertional myocardial ischemia reports discomfort in the chest, jaw, and left upper extremity, despite a normal musculoskeletal examination of the arm. Cardiac and somatic sensory signals travel toward overlapping spinal cord regions. What mechanism explains why the brain experiences some cardiac pain in an uninjured somatic region?","options":["Convergence of visceral and somatic afferents on shared central pathways leads to mislocalization of visceral pain.","Myocardial axons directly innervate left-arm skin.","Direct injury to the left-arm peripheral nerves produces angina.","Local arm inflammation causes secondary coronary artery constriction."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. explains ischemic pain felt in left arm without peripheral arm lesion.","Incorrect. cardiac visceral afferents do not provide somatic arm innervation.","Incorrect. the complaint coincides with ischemia and no nerve injury.","Incorrect. reverses the site and causality."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q49","lecture":7,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"An isolated vertebra has facets for rib articulation on its body and transverse processes, lacks transverse foramina, and has a smaller body than a typical lumbar vertebra. A student must identify its regional origin using these landmarks. Which vertebral region does it belong to?","options":["Lumbar","Thoracic","Cervical","Sacral"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. typical lumbar vertebrae lack costal articular facets.","Correct. articular facets accommodate rib articulations.","Incorrect. typical cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina.","Incorrect. fused sacral segments do not have typical thoracic costal facets."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q50","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A construction worker develops pain after lifting heavy equipment. The physician reviews the posterior ligaments of the vertebral column that resist excessive flexion. One longitudinal ligament joins the tips of successive spinous processes. Which ligament is described?","options":["Anterior longitudinal ligament","Posterior longitudinal ligament","Supraspinous ligament","Ligamentum flavum"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. spans anterior vertebral bodies.","Incorrect. lies behind bodies within canal.","Correct. tracks the tips of spinous processes longitudinally.","Incorrect. bridges adjacent laminae."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q51","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A 51-year-old man develops lower back pain radiating into his posterior calf after lifting a heavy object. Examination reveals weakened ankle plantarflexion and a diminished Achilles reflex. MRI demonstrates a posterolateral disc herniation at L5–S1 impinging on the descending root within the canal while sparing the root exiting through the same-level foramen. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?","options":["Traversing S1 root","Exiting S1 root","Traversing L4 root","Exiting L5 root"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. S1 descends past the L5–S1 disc in the lateral recess and fits plantarflexion/Achilles deficits.","Incorrect. S1 does not exit at the L5–S1 foramen; it continues toward the sacral exit.","Incorrect. L4 root distribution and exit anatomy do not fit this level or deficits.","Incorrect. L5 is the exiting root at L5–S1 and is usually spared by a paracentral protrusion."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q52","lecture":8,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A histology section from the sole shows a thick stratum corneum and a conspicuous clear layer. Immediately beneath the clear layer lie flattened epidermal cells containing prominent basophilic keratohyalin granules. Which epidermal stratum contains these granules?","options":["Stratum spinosum","Stratum granulosum","Stratum corneum","Stratum basale"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Spinosum is notable for desmosomal spines, not its characteristic granular accumulation.","Correct. Granulosum keratinocytes contain keratohyalin granules before cornification.","Incorrect. Corneum comprises anucleate squames above the granular and lucidum layers.","Incorrect. Basale contains mitotically active basal keratinocytes rather than prominent keratohyalin granules."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q53","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"During pregnancy, a woman develops symmetric brown patches on the face that darken with sun exposure. Examination reveals no completely depigmented areas, and the melanocyte population is preserved. Hormonal influences and ultraviolet exposure have increased local pigment production. Which acquired pigmentary disorder is most likely?","options":["Pityriasis alba","Vitiligo","Melasma","Albinism"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Pityriasis alba typically causes subtle hypopigmented scaly facial patches in children.","Incorrect. Vitiligo involves loss of melanocytes and depigmentation rather than pregnancy-related hyperpigmentation.","Correct. Melasma commonly causes UV- and hormone-associated facial hyperpigmentation with melanocytes retained.","Incorrect. Albinism is a usually congenital pigment synthesis disorder rather than acquired facial patches."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q54","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"Two infants have different barrier problems. Patient A shows increased transepidermal water loss without blistering; microscopy demonstrates intact keratinocyte junctions but reduced extracellular lipids in the stratum corneum. Patient B develops friction-induced blisters despite normal lipid secretion, and biopsy identifies failure of basal attachment to the basement membrane. Which paired mechanism best distinguishes them?","options":["A: lost desmosomes; B: reduced corneal lipids","A: impaired keratohyalin synthesis exclusively; B: inability to produce melanin","A: impaired dermal collagen tensile strength; B: defective eccrine sweat secretion","A: defective lamellar-body lipid barrier; B: basal hemidesmosomal adhesion failure"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. This reverses the patterns: A has intact junctions, and B has normal lipid secretion.","Incorrect. Keratohyalin abnormalities may affect cornification but do not explain B's specific basal detachment.","Incorrect. Neither dermal collagen weakness nor sweating explains the paired ultrastructural findings.","Correct. Correct: extracellular lipids limit water loss; hemidesmosomal adhesion limits basal epidermal separation."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q55","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"An older man with chronic venous insufficiency has a painful medial ankle wound. The depressed lesion has lost epidermis and part of the underlying dermis and has persisted for several weeks. The clinician anticipates scarring after healing. Which term best describes this cutaneous lesion?","options":["Ulcer","Excoriation","Fissure","Erosion"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Ulcer extends into dermis and may heal with scar formation.","Incorrect. Excoriation denotes scratch-induced injury rather than the depth-based lesion described.","Incorrect. Fissure is a linear crack, not a broad tissue-loss depression.","Incorrect. Erosion is restricted to superficial epidermal loss and typically heals without scarring."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q56","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A woman has scattered reddish-purple lesions on both lower legs. When a transparent slide is pressed against one, the discoloration remains visible, whereas nearby erythematous skin becomes paler. Which mechanism best explains the persistent color of the nonblanching lesions?","options":["Persistent dilation of vessels containing intravascular blood","Extravasated erythrocytes remaining in tissue after vessel compression","Transient mast-cell-mediated dermal edema","Loss of melanocyte pigment with epidermal whitening"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Intravascular blood generally blanches when vessels are compressed.","Correct. Purpura is blood outside vessels, so pressure cannot empty it.","Incorrect. Dermal edema produces a wheal and is not the mechanism of purple nonblanching spots.","Incorrect. Pigment loss does not create nonblanching red-purple lesions."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q57","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A woman has painful oral erosions and recurrent fragile cutaneous blisters. Routine biopsy suggests intraepidermal loss of keratinocyte adhesion. Her dermatologist wants to visualize immunoglobulin already deposited between keratinocytes in the patient's own skin rather than test circulating serum antibodies. Which diagnostic test and specimen are most appropriate?","options":["Indirect immunofluorescence using serum and donor substrate","Serum ELISA for circulating antigen-specific antibodies","Direct immunofluorescence on perilesional skin","Routine H&E of blister roof without immunostaining"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. IIF reveals circulating antibodies rather than deposits already in patient skin.","Incorrect. ELISA quantifies antigen-specific serum reactivity but not the location of tissue-bound Ig.","Correct. Perilesional DIF detects immunoglobulin in intact adjacent skin while avoiding loss of deposits in a ruptured blister.","Incorrect. H&E identifies cleavage plane and infiltrates but cannot map in-situ antibody deposition."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q58","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A 17-year-old has numerous open and closed facial comedones with only a few inflamed papules. There are no deep nodules or scars. The dermatologist proposes a topical medication that normalizes follicular keratinization and notes that benefit may take several weeks. Which treatment class best targets the process responsible for comedone formation?","options":["Topical antifungal to eradicate dermatophytes","Systemic antihistamine to suppress wheals","Topical vasoconstrictor to treat flushing","Topical retinoid to normalize follicular keratinization"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Dermatophytes do not explain comedones.","Incorrect. Histamine-mediated wheals are unrelated to acne follicular plugging.","Incorrect. Vasoconstriction addresses rosacea-associated erythema, not comedones.","Correct. Retinoids limit microcomedones by normalizing follicular epithelial turnover."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q59","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A woman with chronic hidradenitis suppurativa has widely interconnected axillary and inguinal tunnels, scarring, and continued painful inflammation after an adequate trial of topical and oral antibiotics. Which treatment plan best addresses the combined active inflammation and irreversible tunnel architecture?","options":["Systemic biologic treatment with surgical assessment for removal of extensive tunnels","Another topical antibiotic course without reassessment of sinus tracts","Systemic antibiotics alone with the assumption that fibrotic tunnels will reliably disappear","Long-term topical anti-inflammatory treatment alone to reverse established scarring"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: extensive disease may need systemic control and excision of chronically affected tissue.","Incorrect. Topical antibiotics are insufficient for extensive Hurley III tunnel disease.","Incorrect. Antibiotics can reduce inflammation but do not reliably eliminate fibrotic interconnected tunnels.","Incorrect. Topicals cannot generally remodel extensive permanent tunnel and scar architecture."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q60","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A young woman with autoimmune thyroid disease develops three smooth patches of hair loss without scale or scarring; hairs at the margins are fragile. A child in her household has an itchy scaly patch with broken shafts and a positive fungal study. Both have focal hair loss, but only the first has a follicular inflammatory disorder that typically preserves the potential for regrowth. Which difference in the primary target of injury accounts for these distinct patterns?","options":["Both arise primarily from androgen-driven follicular miniaturization","First reflects T-cell-associated follicular attack with preserved follicles; second reflects dermatophyte invasion and inflammation of hair shafts/follicles","First reflects irreversible follicular scarring; second is caused by autoimmune melanocyte destruction","Both arise from abrupt synchronized transition of follicles to telogen"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Androgenetic alopecia is patterned and gradual, not focal smooth vs scaly infectious patches.","Correct. Alopecia areata is immune mediated and nonscarring; tinea capitis is a fungal infection with scale and broken hairs.","Incorrect. Alopecia areata is usually nonscarring, and melanocyte destruction defines vitiligo, not tinea capitis.","Incorrect. Telogen effluvium produces diffuse post-stressor shedding rather than these focal lesions."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q61","lecture":1,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A radiograph of the knee demonstrates smooth articular cartilage, a joint cavity with synovial fluid, and a surrounding capsule. The articulating bones are not united directly by dense fibrous tissue or hyaline cartilage. By structural classification, what type of joint is present?","options":["Fibrous joint","Cartilaginous joint","Synovial joint","Synostosis"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Fibrous joints lack a fluid-filled cavity.","Incorrect. Cartilaginous joints link bones through cartilage and do not feature a synovial cavity.","Correct. A synovial cavity, articular cartilage and surrounding capsule define this joint class.","Incorrect. Synostosis describes bony fusion without a joint cavity."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q62","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A woman develops persistent unilateral arm swelling after axillary lymph-node dissection. Duplex ultrasonography shows patent veins and normal arterial delivery, but interstitial fluid and proteins accumulate in the affected limb. Which normal transport function was compromised by removal of the lymphatic drainage pathway?","options":["Diffusion of oxygen across epidermal cells","Venous valvular closure preventing reflux","Arterial blood delivery to capillaries","Lymphatic uptake and return of interstitial protein and excess fluid"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Oxygen diffusion does not remove interstitial proteins or explain limb swelling.","Incorrect. Venous patency argues against venous obstruction as the primary cause.","Incorrect. Arterial delivery is preserved and is not the impaired interstitial-clearance route.","Correct. Lymphatic interruption blocks return of excess interstitial water and proteins, promoting lymphedema."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q63","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"During resisted elbow flexion, a patient maintains normal agonist torque but loses control of shoulder position as fatigue develops. EMG shows newly recruited muscles spanning the shoulder, which restrict unwanted shoulder movement while elbow flexion force remains unchanged. Which functional description most specifically matches these recruited muscles?","options":["Fixators that stabilize an adjacent joint without materially augmenting the agonist's primary movement","Neutralizers that generate an equal opposing elbow-flexion torque","Agonists replacing the elbow flexor as the prime source of torque","Antagonists whose principal action is to produce elbow extension"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: stabilization of the shoulder permits the elbow agonist to act without undesirable proximal motion.","Incorrect. Neutralization can cancel a component of force but an equal opposing elbow-flexion torque is not the described stabilization.","Incorrect. The stem says primary elbow-flexion torque is unchanged and supplied by the original agonist.","Incorrect. Extension antagonism would oppose elbow flexion, not specifically stabilize the shoulder."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q64","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A therapist uses force measurements during two ankle exercises. In a standing heel raise, the metatarsal heads act as a fulcrum, body weight acts between the fulcrum and the Achilles tendon force, and the patient slowly rises. In a different exercise, the external resistance is relocated farther from the fulcrum without changing its magnitude. The patient must now produce more muscular force to achieve the same angular acceleration. Which change in the relative moment arms accounts for the increased force requirement despite the unchanged load?","options":["External torque increases because resistance has a longer perpendicular moment arm, while required muscle torque rises to match it.","The external torque remains constant because the resistance force has not changed; only mechanical work increases.","The greater muscle-force requirement results from a smaller resistance moment arm and unchanged effort moment arm.","Muscle force rises because the fulcrum necessarily migrates to the center of the resistance during a heel raise."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Moment arm increases external torque for a fixed force; muscles must counter the increased torque.","Incorrect. External torque changes with the perpendicular distance to the joint axis even at constant force.","Incorrect. A smaller resistance moment arm would reduce the external torque of a fixed resistance.","Incorrect. The described fulcrum remains at the forefoot; load relocation changes its moment arm, not the fulcrum definition."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q65","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A man with peripheral arterial disease has a tight proximal arterial stenosis but several smaller distal vessel-to-vessel connections. His foot is warm and adequately perfused at rest, yet walking causes calf pain and inadequate oxygen delivery. Which distinction between anatomical anastomoses and functionally adequate collateral circulation explains this pattern?","options":["Venous valves are essential for increasing arterial collateral flow","An anastomosis guarantees normal perfusion during any level of exertion","Patent collateral connections may have insufficient conductance to meet exercise demand","Normal resting temperature excludes flow-limiting arterial disease"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Venous valves are not the limiting mechanism in arterial collateral resistance.","Incorrect. Presence of connections does not guarantee adequate flow reserve.","Correct. Collateral conductance may sustain resting flow yet fail when metabolic demand rises.","Incorrect. Resting warmth does not exclude exertional ischemia."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q66","lecture":2,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"In spinal cord gray matter, a student identifies a region filled with large multipolar neuronal somata whose axons leave through ventral roots to activate skeletal muscle. Which gray-matter horn contains these neurons?","options":["Posterior funiculus","Dorsal horn","Lateral funiculus","Ventral horn"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Posterior funiculus contains ascending sensory axons.","Incorrect. Dorsal horn processes sensory input.","Incorrect. Lateral funiculus contains tracts rather than the described group of motor somata.","Correct. Ventral horn gray matter houses lower motor neuron cell bodies."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q67","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A middle-aged adult develops burning pain followed by unilateral grouped vesicles in a narrow band at the umbilical level. The affected skin has increased sensitivity and the distribution follows a single spinal sensory segment. Which dermatome corresponds most closely to this landmark?","options":["T10","L1","T4","T6"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. T10 classically corresponds to the umbilicus.","Incorrect. L1 is more inferior, associated with inguinal region.","Incorrect. T4 is associated with nipple level.","Incorrect. T6 is higher than the umbilicus, near the xiphoid region."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q68","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A 62-year-old patient has new retention of urine, loss of perineal sensation, and asymmetric distal weakness in several lumbar and sacral myotomes. Achilles and patellar reflexes are diminished, and MRI shows a large central L3–L4 disc extrusion. Which localization best accounts for a multiroot lower-motor-neuron syndrome below the termination of the adult spinal cord?","options":["Bilateral dorsal-column injury at T10","Compression of multiple descending lumbosacral roots within the cauda equina","A conus lesion confined to the spinal cord at L4","Isolated injury of one S1 dorsal root ganglion"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Dorsal-column injury would chiefly impair vibration/proprioception, not cause multiroot LMN weakness and retention.","Correct. Correct: multiple roots descend through the lumbar canal below the conus and may be compressed together.","Incorrect. The adult conus usually ends near L1–L2 and is not a spinal cord structure at L4.","Incorrect. One sensory ganglion cannot produce broad bilateral motor and autonomic deficits."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q69","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"Following descending-aortic surgery, a patient develops bilateral weakness and impaired pain and temperature below the thoracic level, while vibration and proprioception remain comparatively intact. MRI demonstrates spinal cord ischemia rather than an isolated root lesion. Which vascular territory and tract distribution account for the sensory dissociation?","options":["A unilateral spinal root injury produces symmetric bilateral long-tract signs","Posterior spinal arteries chiefly supply the infarcted anterolateral territory","Anterior spinal artery supplies motor and pain-temperature pathways, with dorsal columns often spared","Anterior spinal artery selectively supplies only posterior column pathways"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Unilateral radiculopathy cannot account for bilateral tract-selective losses below a cord level.","Incorrect. Posterior vessels preferentially supply dorsal regions, not the pattern of ventral dysfunction.","Correct. ASA territory includes anterior two thirds with corticospinal and anterolateral pathways; posterior columns may remain.","Incorrect. Posterior column preservation contradicts a posterior-only ASA distribution."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q70","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"Two patients have posterior trunk sensory loss at the same lumbar level. One also has weakness of intrinsic back muscles but preserved limb strength, while the other has sensory impairment throughout most of the corresponding dermatome and no motor deficit. The first injury occurs outside the spinal canal after formation of the mixed spinal nerve; the second is located proximally before sensory fibers join their motor counterparts. Which pairing of injured structures best explains the contrasting distributions?","options":["First: ventral root; second: ventral ramus","First: dorsal root; second: dorsal ramus","First: ventral ramus; second: ventral root","First: dorsal ramus; second: dorsal root"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Ventral root is motor-only, and ventral ramus targets limb/anterolateral trunk, inconsistent with patterns.","Incorrect. This reverses the motor-plus-skin versus sensory-only distribution.","Incorrect. Neither structure explains the specified posterior-trunk and dermatome patterns.","Correct. The mixed dorsal ramus supplies intrinsic back muscles and posterior skin; a dorsal-root lesion causes sensory-only segmental loss."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q71","lecture":3,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"Potassium-selective channels connect solutions with different potassium concentrations. A transmembrane voltage develops until electrical force opposes diffusion sufficiently that there is no net flux of potassium. What is the name of the membrane voltage associated with this ionic balance?","options":["Equilibrium (Nernst) potential","Afterhyperpolarization potential","Action potential overshoot","Threshold potential"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. At the equilibrium potential electrical and chemical driving forces for one ion balance so net flux is zero.","Incorrect. Afterhyperpolarization is a transient post-spike voltage.","Incorrect. Overshoot refers to positive membrane potential during the action-potential peak.","Incorrect. Threshold is the membrane voltage sufficient to initiate a regenerative action potential."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q72","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A neuron receives small excitatory postsynaptic potentials from several different axon terminals at nearly the same time. No single input reaches threshold, but their combined depolarizations trigger an action potential. Which mechanism of neural integration is responsible?","options":["Saltatory conduction","Spatial summation","Absolute refractoriness","Temporal summation"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Saltatory conduction describes myelinated axonal propagation, not integration of synapses.","Correct. Spatial summation adds inputs from different presynaptic sites.","Incorrect. Refractoriness is a period of limited action-potential excitability after firing.","Incorrect. Temporal summation repeats activity from one synaptic input over time."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q73","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"In a voltage-clamp experiment, an agent blocks delayed rectifier potassium channels but preserves the transient sodium current. Relative to control fibers, action potentials rise normally but remain positive longer, and sodium channels recover more slowly for subsequent stimulation. Which change in ionic conductance most directly generates this paired observation?","options":["Increased potassium leak that shifts the resting potential toward EK","Loss of ligand-gated excitatory sodium current at the synapse","Reduced delayed outward potassium conductance, prolonging depolarization and sodium-channel inactivation","Reduced voltage-gated sodium activation, selectively flattening the upstroke"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Increased potassium leak would generally favor a more negative potential, not a prolonged overshoot.","Incorrect. The neuron is directly stimulated and the action-potential upstroke is intact.","Correct. Correct: delayed potassium efflux mediates repolarization; slowing it prolongs sodium-channel inactivation.","Incorrect. The preserved upstroke argues against impaired voltage-gated sodium activation."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q74","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"After prolonged gastrointestinal fluid losses, a patient develops hypokalemia. Muscle membrane recordings reveal a resting voltage more negative than normal, with potassium leak conductance still predominant. How does the fall in extracellular potassium alter its equilibrium potential and thereby resting membrane voltage?","options":["Lower extracellular K+ makes EK less negative and depolarizes Vm","Hypokalemia reverses Na+/K+ ATPase transport direction","Hypokalemia directly forces voltage-gated Na+ channels permanently open","Lower extracellular K+ makes EK more negative and shifts Vm negative"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. This describes a high-extracellular-potassium direction instead.","Incorrect. ATPase direction does not reverse as a direct consequence of low serum K+.","Incorrect. Voltage-gated Na+ channels are not forced open as a necessary mechanism of hypokalemia.","Correct. Nernst relationship: lower extracellular K+ lowers EK, and K+-dominant resting Vm becomes more negative."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q75","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A patient with inflammatory demyelination develops slowed impulse conduction and intermittent propagation failure. Electrophysiology shows that action potentials can begin in an intact proximal region, while myelin is lost farther along the same axon. Which change in passive current movement across formerly insulated internodes best explains the failure to excite downstream nodes?","options":["Increased current loss along exposed internode so downstream nodes fail to reach threshold","Loss of all resting K+ gradients despite intact ion pumps","Irreversible replacement of axons with connective tissue at every affected site","Reduced membrane leakage and accelerated node-to-node depolarization"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Demyelination increases current leak and membrane capacitance, reducing effective depolarization of the next node.","Incorrect. No wholesale ion-gradient loss is described.","Incorrect. Underlying axons are partly preserved, not universally replaced.","Incorrect. Intact myelin prevents leakage and supports rapid propagation; its loss does the opposite."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q76","lecture":4,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A healing wound contains spindle-shaped cells with abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum synthesizing collagen and extracellular matrix proteins. Which resident connective tissue cell is chiefly responsible for this synthesis?","options":["Mast cell","Fibroblast","Macrophage","White adipocyte"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Mast cells release mediators but are not major collagen producers.","Correct. Active fibroblasts synthesize and deposit the collagen-rich matrix of wound repair.","Incorrect. Macrophages clear debris and coordinate inflammation rather than being the principal collagen-producing cells.","Incorrect. White adipocytes store triglyceride and are not the primary scar matrix source."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q77","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Shortly after an insect sting, a patient develops local itching, redness, and edema. Tissue-resident granular connective tissue cells release mediators that rapidly increase vascular permeability. Which cell type is responsible?","options":["Neutrophil","Fibrocyte","Mast cell","Adipocyte"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Neutrophils are recruited inflammatory cells, not the typical resident histamine-granule source.","Incorrect. Fibrocytes are relatively inactive connective tissue cells and do not produce histamine wheals.","Correct. Mast-cell degranulation releases histamine, increasing vascular permeability and edema.","Incorrect. Adipocytes have metabolic and cushioning roles, not acute histamine release."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q78","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Two fibroblast cultures produce similar amounts of triple-helical procollagen and secrete it into the medium. In one, retained terminal extensions prevent close packing and normal formation of extracellular collagen fibrils. In the other, normal fibrils form. Which enzyme-dependent extracellular maturation event is defective in the first culture?","options":["Lysyl hydroxylation during translation in the rough endoplasmic reticulum","Extracellular fibril cross-linking after normal propeptide removal","Intracellular glycosylation before secretion","Extracellular procollagen N- and C-propeptide cleavage"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Hydroxylation occurs intracellularly; the secreted triple helix is already formed.","Incorrect. Cross-linking is later; the retained terminal propeptides identify an earlier extracellular processing failure.","Incorrect. Intracellular glycosylation cannot directly account for terminal extensions persisting after secretion.","Correct. Correct: procollagen peptidase processing allows mature tropocollagen to pack into fibrils."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q79","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A patient has hematuria, sensorineural hearing impairment, and ultrastructural abnormalities of the glomerular basement membrane. His dermal fibrillar collagen remains intact. Which combination of collagen architecture and normal location most directly accounts for the affected renal barrier?","options":["Type IV collagen assembled into a sheet-like basement-membrane network","Type I collagen arranged in parallel tendon-like fibrils","Type III collagen organized as reticular fibers in hematopoietic tissue","Type II collagen assembled in pressure-resistant cartilage fibrils"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: type IV collagen forms interconnected networks in basal laminae, including glomerular basement membranes.","Incorrect. Type I fibrillar collagen supports tendon and dermis but is not the specialized glomerular basal-lamina network.","Incorrect. Reticular type III collagen supports soft tissues, not the primary basement membrane collagen network.","Incorrect. Type II collagen resists compression in cartilage, not glomerular filtration basal laminae."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q80","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Following comparable injuries, a tendon heals with a collagen-rich bridge while an infarcted region of myocardium is replaced by a similarly collagen-rich scar. Both sites achieve structural continuity, but only the cardiac region loses substantial active mechanical function. Which tissue-specific difference best explains the divergence?","options":["New collagen supplies the full active contractile function of both tissues","Collagen can transfer tensile load in tendon but cannot replace cardiomyocyte force generation","Healthy cardiac muscle normally lacks collagen while tendons contain only collagen","Scar formation selectively blocks electrical activity without affecting ventricular contraction"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Collagen provides passive structural support; it cannot replace active cardiac muscle contraction.","Correct. Correct: tendon function relies substantially on tensile force transfer, while heart muscle requires living contractile myocytes.","Incorrect. The heart has extracellular matrix and tendons have cellular components; neither absolute claim is correct.","Incorrect. Scar tissue affects mechanical performance directly, not solely electrical conduction."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q81","lecture":5,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A neuron must deliver newly synthesized synaptic vesicle components from its cell body to an axon terminal located far from the soma. Fluorescent labeling shows the cargo moving along microtubules toward the terminal rather than returning to the cell body. When one motor protein is inhibited, this outward transport stops, although the microtubule tracks remain intact. Which molecular motor normally drives this movement?","options":["Tubulin","Dynein","Kinesin","Myosin II"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Tubulin forms microtubules but is not the motor protein.","Incorrect. Dynein usually mediates retrograde axonal transport to soma.","Correct. Kinesin drives anterograde cargo from soma toward axon terminals.","Incorrect. Myosin II is not the characteristic long-distance microtubule cargo motor."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q82","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A biopsy of a sensory structure near the spinal column demonstrates large neurons with round cell bodies surrounded by nearly complete rings of small supporting cell nuclei. The neurons have a single bifurcating process, and synapses between the principal cells are absent. A second specimen from an autonomic ganglion instead contains scattered multipolar neurons. Which structure is represented by the first specimen?","options":["Anterior horn of cord","Sympathetic chain white ramus","Autonomic ganglion","Dorsal root ganglion"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Anterior horn is CNS gray matter, not a peripheral ganglion with satellite cuffs.","Incorrect. A white ramus is a connecting nerve branch, not a neuronal ganglion.","Incorrect. Autonomic ganglia have multipolar neurons and less complete satellite cuffs with synapses.","Correct. DRG contains pseudounipolar primary sensory cells with satellite-cell cuffs and no local synapses."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q83","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A patient sustains a laceration that transects a peripheral nerve. Over the following days, axons distal to the injury fragment, and the affected neuronal cell bodies become swollen with redistribution of basophilic rough endoplasmic reticulum. The latter change occurs within the soma rather than the injured nerve segment. Which neuronal injury response is demonstrated?","options":["Somatic chromatolysis with dispersal of Nissl substance","New compact myelin synthesis by the neuronal nucleus","Conversion of pseudounipolar cells into bipolar neurons","Formation of a blood–nerve barrier within neuronal cytoplasm"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Axonal injury can trigger chromatolysis: swelling and dispersion of Nissl rough ER in the cell body.","Incorrect. Myelin is made by glia, not by the neuron nucleus.","Incorrect. The neuronal morphological class does not generally switch in this way.","Incorrect. Perineurial cells form the blood–nerve barrier outside neurons."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q84","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A toxin damages myelinating glia in an experimental CNS tract and affects several internodes distributed across different axons around each injured glial cell. In a separately treated peripheral nerve, glial-cell death causes loss of one compact-myelin internode at a time. Axons are initially preserved in both preparations. Which anatomical difference in how glial cells contact axons explains the contrasting pattern of segmental myelin loss?","options":["Both glia normally wrap exactly one segment of one axon","CNS oligodendrocytes can myelinate multiple axon internodes; PNS Schwann cells one myelinated internode each","PNS Schwann cells myelinate every axon in a spinal tract","CNS oligodendrocytes form the perineurial blood–nerve barrier"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. This describes PNS compact myelin but not CNS oligodendrocyte branching.","Correct. One oligodendrocyte sends processes to multiple CNS segments while one Schwann makes one myelinated internode.","Incorrect. Schwann cells are peripheral and do not myelinate spinal cord tracts.","Incorrect. Perineurial cells form the fascicular barrier, not oligodendrocytes."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q85","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A man develops distal leg wasting and pes cavus over more than a decade, with similar findings in two generations of his family. Another patient becomes unable to walk over several days after a gastrointestinal infection, with rapidly progressive ascending weakness and generalized areflexia. Nerve studies confirm peripheral neuropathy in both patients. Which combination of disease time course, inherited versus acquired mechanism, and neural injury pattern most strongly separates the two conditions?","options":["Both patients have acute immune-mediated segmental demyelination, but the first has inherited abnormal foot structure.","The first presentation favors a chronic hereditary peripheral neuropathy; the second favors an acute postinfectious immune neuropathy.","The first presentation favors an acute viral myelopathy; the second favors a chronic inherited dorsal-column degeneration.","The first indicates an isolated lumbar radiculopathy; the second indicates focal common peroneal entrapment."],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. The first has a longstanding, familial progressive neuropathy, not an acute postinfectious syndrome.","Correct. Chronic familial distal weakness and pes cavus support hereditary neuropathy; acute postinfectious ascending weakness supports GBS.","Incorrect. Both patterns are primarily peripheral nerve disorders with distinct time courses.","Incorrect. Bilateral progressive familial symptoms and rapidly ascending areflexic weakness are not explained by those focal lesions."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q86","lecture":6,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A patient develops altered bladder and distal bowel autonomic function after a sacral neurological injury. Testing indicates disruption of preganglionic parasympathetic outflow rather than sympathetic fibers from the thoracolumbar cord. The clinician reviews the spinal origin of the nerves that normally supply these pelvic organs. Which spinal levels contain the relevant parasympathetic preganglionic neurons?","options":["L4–S1","T1–L2","C1–C4","S2–S4"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. L4–S1 is not the classic sacral parasympathetic origin.","Incorrect. T1–L2 is sympathetic origin.","Incorrect. Cervical spinal segments do not define the sacral parasympathetic component.","Correct. S2–S4 contains sacral parasympathetic preganglionic outflow."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q87","lecture":6,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"During dissection of the sympathetic trunk, a student compares the two communicating branches that link the trunk with spinal nerves. One carries preganglionic fibers to the trunk only at thoracolumbar levels, while the other conveys sympathetic fibers back to spinal nerves throughout the body. Which branch is found at essentially all spinal levels?","options":["Gray rami communicantes","Dorsal roots","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","White rami communicantes"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Gray rami carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers back to spinal nerves at all spinal levels.","Incorrect. Dorsal roots carry sensory afferents, not the specified sympathetic-return branch.","Incorrect. Pelvic splanchnic nerves are sacral parasympathetic pathways.","Incorrect. White rami are limited to thoracolumbar sympathetic levels."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q88","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"During pelvic surgery, a patient sustains injury to sensory fibers carrying pain from a pelvic organ located below the pelvic pain line. The surgeon explains that these pain afferents ordinarily travel alongside sacral parasympathetic pathways on their way to sensory ganglia. Which spinal segmental pathway is most directly involved?","options":["T1–L2 sympathetic sensory return","S2–S4 dorsal root ganglia alongside pelvic parasympathetic pathways","Cervical sympathetic ganglia","Only intramural enteric neurons without any spinal sensory return"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Above-line visceral pain often accompanies sympathetics toward T1–L2, not below line.","Correct. Below the pelvic pain line afferents typically accompany pelvic parasympathetics toward S2–S4 DRGs.","Incorrect. Cervical ganglia do not receive this pelvic sensory pathway.","Incorrect. Conscious visceral pain reaches spinal sensory neurons, not exclusively intrinsic enteric networks."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q89","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"An abdominal surgeon identifies a sympathetic ganglion near the abdominal aorta receiving input from fibers that originated in thoracolumbar spinal cord segments. Those fibers entered the sympathetic trunk but did not synapse there, and postganglionic fibers from this ganglion follow arteries to the viscera. Which route through the sympathetic trunk allows the preganglionic fibers to reach their ganglion?","options":["Leave CNS directly by dorsal root and synapse in skin","Enter pelvic splanchnic nerves carrying sacral parasympathetic outflow","Enter trunk by white ramus, pass without synapsing, then travel in abdominopelvic splanchnic to prevertebral ganglion","Synapse in sympathetic trunk then travel by gray ramus into limb skin"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Dorsal roots are afferent rather than efferent motor pathways.","Incorrect. Pelvic splanchnics carry parasympathetic S2–S4 fibers, not thoracolumbar sympathetic preganglionics.","Correct. Abdominal sympathetic preganglionics can bypass chain synapses and synapse in prevertebral ganglia via splanchnic nerves.","Incorrect. Gray ramus is for postganglionic supply to body wall, not this prevertebral target."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q90","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A patient has reduced intestinal propulsion but retains near-normal mucosal secretion. A second patient has markedly abnormal fluid secretion into the lumen and impaired local mucosal blood-flow regulation, while propulsive contractions remain coordinated. Both have intact vagal and sacral parasympathetic connections. Which distribution of injury between the two intrinsic enteric plexuses best explains these dissociated physiological deficits?","options":["Both are confined to sympathetic trunk ganglia","Both are composed exclusively of sensory dorsal root neurons","Meissner for propulsion and Auerbach for mucosal secretion","Auerbach for propulsion and Meissner for secretion and local flow"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. These plexuses are within gut wall, not sympathetic trunk.","Incorrect. Enteric networks include intrinsic neurons and are not solely DRG sensory neurons.","Incorrect. Reverses typical functional emphasis of the enteric plexuses.","Correct. Myenteric Auerbach regulates motility; submucosal Meissner regulates mucosal secretions and local flow."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q91","lecture":7,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A cervical vertebral specimen consists of anterior and posterior arches and prominent lateral masses. It lacks the conventional body and spinous process of a typical vertebra. In the intact patient, this bone helps support the skull at the joint involved in nodding. Which vertebra is being examined?","options":["C1 atlas","T1 vertebra","C2 axis","C7 vertebra prominens"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. C1 forms a ring without typical body or spinous process and supports nodding at atlanto-occipital joint.","Incorrect. T1 has thoracic features including rib articulation facets.","Incorrect. C2 bears the dens and vertebral body.","Incorrect. C7 has a prominent spinous process."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q92","lecture":7,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A 47-year-old man undergoes MRI for chronic axial lower back pain. The radiologist points to the hydrated central region of an intervertebral disc, which distributes compression and is enclosed by concentric fibrocartilaginous tissue. Which named disc structure provides this central load-distributing function?","options":["Annulus fibrosus","Nucleus pulposus","Posterior longitudinal ligament","Ligamentum flavum"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Annulus is the outer fibrocartilage containing the nucleus.","Correct. Nucleus pulposus is the hydrated central region for load distribution.","Incorrect. PLL runs posterior to vertebral bodies within canal.","Incorrect. Ligamentum flavum connects adjacent laminae."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q93","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"Following a localized penetrating injury near the lumbar spinous processes, a patient develops weakness of the deep back muscles responsible for segmental spinal stabilization. Strength of the large extrinsic shoulder and scapular muscles is preserved. Which subdivision of the spinal nerves normally provides motor innervation to the injured intrinsic muscles?","options":["Ventral rami","Dorsal roots","Posterior (dorsal) rami","Spinal accessory nerve"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Ventral rami supply most anterolateral trunk/limb regions.","Incorrect. Dorsal roots carry sensory afferents, not efferent innervation.","Correct. Intrinsic epaxial back muscles receive motor fibers from posterior rami.","Incorrect. Accessory nerve innervates trapezius, a superficial extrinsic back muscle."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q94","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A patient has weakness rotating the head leftward when the right-sided deep cervical extensors are selectively tested. Bilateral extension remains largely intact, and ipsilateral-rotating superficial muscles are preserved. The examiner suspects a deep intrinsic muscle group that produces rotation toward the opposite side during unilateral contraction. Which muscle group is most likely involved?","options":["Right semispinalis, which rotates the head contralaterally toward the left.","Right splenius, which rotates the head contralaterally toward the left.","Left semispinalis, which rotates the head ipsilaterally toward the left.","Right erector spinae, whose defining unilateral function is contralateral cervical rotation."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. The semispinalis group produces contralateral rotation during unilateral contraction.","Incorrect. Unilateral splenius contraction rotates the head ipsilaterally.","Incorrect. Semispinalis rotates contralaterally; the left side tends to rotate to the right.","Incorrect. Erector spinae primarily extends and laterally flexes rather than supplying the hallmark semispinalis rotation pattern."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q95","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"Two young athletes undergo lumbar radiography for extension-related pain. One has a defect in the bone connecting a vertebra's superior and inferior articular processes, but normal alignment. The other has similar defects and measurable anterior vertebral displacement relative to the segment below. Which added mechanical consequence distinguishes the second patient's condition from the first rather than merely repeating the shared bone lesion?","options":["Presence of anterior vertebral translation distinguishes spondylolisthesis from isolated spondylolysis","Spinal stenosis is mandatory whenever a pars defect exists","A pars defect describes a fracture of the dens","Pars defect alone defines spondylolisthesis without displacement"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Spondylolisthesis adds positional slippage of a vertebra relative to another.","Incorrect. Canal stenosis may coexist but is not definitionally required.","Incorrect. Pars lies between vertebral articular processes, not within the dens.","Incorrect. A pars defect alone is spondylolysis, without required slippage."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q96","lecture":8,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A biopsy from hair-bearing scalp skin contains lobulated glands opening into hair follicles. Cells accumulate lipid as they migrate toward the glandular lumen and ultimately disintegrate, releasing their contents along with cellular debris. Which gland and secretion mechanism produce this material?","options":["Apocrine sweat gland: apocrine","Sebaceous gland: holocrine","Goblet cell: merocrine","Eccrine gland: merocrine"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Apocrine glands are not the described hair-follicle-associated pale lipid lobules.","Correct. Sebaceous cells disintegrate to release sebum, defining holocrine secretion.","Incorrect. Goblet cells secrete mucin, not lipid-rich sebum by complete cell breakdown.","Incorrect. Eccrine sweat glands release product without whole-cell destruction."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q97","lecture":8,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A neurologist examines a fingertip biopsy from a patient with selective loss of fine light-touch discrimination. Small encapsulated nerve endings are visible within dermal papillae immediately under the epidermis, whereas deep pressure receptors are preserved. Which named receptor corresponds to the damaged superficial structures?","options":["Muscle spindle","Pacinian corpuscle","Meissner corpuscle","Free nerve ending only"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Muscle spindles lie in skeletal muscle and detect stretch.","Incorrect. Pacinian corpuscles lie deeper and detect pressure/vibration.","Correct. Meissner corpuscles in papillary dermis mediate superficial discriminative touch.","Incorrect. Free endings have different morphology and often convey pain/temperature."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q98","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A child is evaluated after repeat sweat testing reveals excessive salt on the skin surface. The secretory coils of the eccrine glands remain functional, but their ducts fail to reclaim chloride efficiently, disrupting associated sodium reabsorption. Which ductal transport defect best accounts for the observed sweat composition?","options":["Excess melanocyte secretion into gland lumen","Complete failure of sweat secretion by eccrine coils","Increased epidermal sweat evaporation alone","Impaired ductal Cl− reabsorption, with reduced NaCl recovery, causing salty surface sweat"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Melanocytes do not control eccrine chloride flux.","Incorrect. A total absence of sweat differs from elevated salt concentration in produced sweat.","Incorrect. Evaporation may change concentration but does not describe inherited duct chloride transport defect.","Correct. CFTR-related failure to absorb chloride impairs ductal salt recovery so sweat chloride stays elevated."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q99","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"Two adolescents have clinically pale skin. One has lifelong generalized hypopigmentation, visual findings and normal numbers of basal melanocytes; the other has acquired completely white patches and a history of autoimmune thyroid disease. Which pairing of cellular events best explains the different patterns?","options":["Impaired melanin biosynthesis with preserved melanocytes; destruction of melanocytes in affected patches","Loss of melanocytes in both cases, one congenital and the other acquired","Hormone-driven melanin overproduction; retained but inactive melanosomes","Loss of epidermal desmosomes; defect of dermal collagen VII"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: congenital albinism-like disorders impair pigment synthesis with cells retained; vitiligo involves melanocyte destruction.","Incorrect. The first biopsy contradicts absence of melanocytes.","Incorrect. Melanin overproduction causes hyperpigmentation rather than the first patient's appearance.","Incorrect. Junctional and collagen VII defects principally cause skin fragility, not the two pigment disorders."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q100","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"Two fingertip wounds are followed after healing. A superficial abrasion regains the prior ridge pattern; a deeper injury entering the papillary dermis heals with a distorted fingerprint despite complete resurfacing. Which feature of the deeper injury most directly explains this permanent pattern change?","options":["Loss of stratum corneum cells, which define the permanent ridges independently of the dermis","Disruption and scarring of the interlocking epidermal ridges and dermal papillae that normally pattern friction skin","Increased dermal blood flow, which permanently rearranges sweat duct openings","Destruction of hypodermal fat alone despite preservation of papillary dermis"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Surface corneocytes are renewed and do not alone encode stable friction ridge architecture.","Correct. Correct: preserved basal–papillary architecture allows normal resurfacing; deep architectural injury can scar the ridge pattern.","Incorrect. Blood flow does not reorganize fingerprints into a stable new architecture.","Incorrect. Hypodermal fat is not the primary determinant of epidermal friction ridge geometry."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q101","lecture":9,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A 24-year-old patient with chronic atopic dermatitis repeatedly rubs an itchy patch on the forearm for several months. The resulting lesion is thick, with accentuated skin markings and a roughened surface; there is no open wound or loss of dermal tissue. Which secondary skin change best describes the chronic rubbing injury?","options":["Lichenification","Excoriation","Erosion","Atrophy"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Chronic rubbing produces epidermal thickening with accentuated skin markings.","Incorrect. An excoriation is a scratch-induced break in the surface, not diffuse thickening.","Incorrect. An erosion is loss of epidermis and does not explain prominent skin markings.","Incorrect. Atrophy thins rather than thickens skin."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q102","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A 73-year-old woman presents with several tense fluid-filled skin lesions measuring approximately 2 cm in diameter. The lesions contain clear fluid rather than a solid mass or transient edema, and each exceeds the conventional size threshold for a vesicle. Which primary morphology term is most accurate?","options":["Vesicle","Bulla","Wheal","Nodule"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. A vesicle is the smaller fluid-filled lesion and does not fit the 2-cm size.","Correct. A fluid-filled primary lesion greater than 1 cm is a bulla, regardless of the underlying disorder.","Incorrect. A wheal is transient dermal edema rather than a true blister cavity.","Incorrect. A nodule is a solid palpable lesion, not fluid filled."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q103","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A 49-year-old patient develops superficial crusting and easily ruptured skin blisters involving the scalp and upper trunk but no oral erosions. Biopsy shows separation in the upper epidermis, and antibodies are directed against an epidermal cell-to-cell adhesion molecule that predominates superficially. Which protein is the most likely target?","options":["Desmoglein 3","Collagen XVII (BP180)","Desmoglein 1","Type VII collagen"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Dsg3 is especially associated with mucosal pemphigus vulgaris.","Incorrect. BP180 is a hemidesmosomal antigen associated with subepidermal pemphigoid.","Correct. Superficial epidermal blistering without mucosal disease is consistent with Dsg1-dominant adhesion loss.","Incorrect. Type VII collagen anchors basement membrane structures and is implicated in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q104","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A man with gluten-sensitive enteropathy develops grouped intensely pruritic lesions on extensor surfaces. Biopsy of an excoriated blister is nondiagnostic. Which immunofluorescence result from an appropriately selected biopsy would best distinguish the suspected disease from bullous pemphigoid?","options":["Linear IgG at the dermal–epidermal junction in blister-edge tissue","Intercellular IgG between keratinocytes in lesional epidermis","Granular IgA measured only by indirect testing of circulating antibodies","Granular IgA at dermal papillary tips in perilesional skin"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. A linear basement-membrane IgG pattern favors bullous pemphigoid.","Incorrect. Intercellular IgG favors pemphigus vulgaris.","Incorrect. The desired result is tissue-bound deposits shown by direct immunofluorescence, not only serum antibody testing.","Correct. Correct: dermatitis herpetiformis shows granular papillary IgA in perilesional tissue."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q105","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A 58-year-old woman has refractory mucosal erosions, acantholysis and intercellular IgG deposition in the epidermis. She receives rituximab, after which circulating pathogenic antibodies decline. Which paired target distinction explains how the drug helps without directly repairing desmosomes?","options":["CD20 on B cells is targeted therapeutically, while epidermal desmogleins are attacked in disease","Rituximab binds desmoglein 3 directly, restoring keratinocyte adhesion","The drug binds collagen XVII on basal keratinocytes and blocks complement","Rituximab directly binds IgA in dermal papillae and inactivates neutrophils"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: B-cell depletion can reduce production of anti-desmoglein antibodies.","Incorrect. Rituximab binds CD20, not desmoglein 3.","Incorrect. Collagen XVII is associated with bullous pemphigoid, not the drug's target.","Incorrect. Papillary IgA and neutrophils suggest dermatitis herpetiformis, and rituximab has a different mechanism."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q106","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A patient has persistent centrofacial flushing with telangiectasias and no comedones; another has fixed irregular nasal tissue enlargement with relatively few papules. Both are diagnosed with rosacea. Which pairing best distinguishes the predominant process and the treatment directed toward it?","options":["Both are comedonal acne requiring topical retinoids","Neurovascular erythema managed with trigger/vascular-directed care; fixed phymatous tissue treated with procedural remodeling","Both reflect sebaceous infection and should receive antibiotics alone","The vascular pattern requires tissue excision, whereas fixed nasal hypertrophy responds to trigger avoidance alone"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Rosacea lacks the characteristic comedones of acne and fixed hypertrophy is not corrected by retinoids.","Correct. Correct: flushing/telangiectasia and established fibrotic tissue enlargement require different approaches.","Incorrect. Infection alone does not explain the vascular and fibrotic rosacea phenotypes.","Incorrect. The treatment emphasis is reversed."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q107","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A 22-year-old man has excessive bilateral palmar sweating that began during adolescence, interferes with schoolwork, and stops during sleep. Thyroid function is normal and no secondary cause is identified. The clinician recommends a topical agent that physically obstructs the eccrine duct as an initial treatment. Which treatment is most appropriate?","options":["Oral terbinafine","Topical tretinoin","Aluminum chloride","Topical ivermectin"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. This antifungal treats dermatophyte infection rather than sweat production.","Incorrect. A retinoid normalizes follicular keratinization but does not obstruct eccrine ducts.","Correct. Aluminum chloride antiperspirant formulations obstruct eccrine duct outflow and are a first-line option for focal primary hyperhidrosis.","Incorrect. Ivermectin is used for papulopustular rosacea and does not treat sweat duct flow."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q108","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"An 11-year-old boy has a pruritic, scaly patch of scalp hair loss with broken shafts. His sibling has a similar eruption, and fungal microscopy supports dermatophyte involvement of the hair follicle. The clinician explains that treatment applied only to the surface is unlikely to eradicate infected hair shafts. Which route of antifungal treatment is generally required?","options":["Topical antifungal cream alone","Intralesional corticosteroid alone","Topical benzoyl peroxide alone","Oral antifungal therapy"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Cream alone usually fails to clear infection within hair follicles and shafts.","Incorrect. Steroid treatment addresses immune inflammation, not the fungal infection.","Incorrect. This targets acne-associated follicular lesions rather than dermatophytes.","Correct. Hair-shaft and follicular dermatophyte infection generally requires systemic antifungal treatment to reach infected hair."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q109","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A woman with inflammatory jawline acne that worsens premenstrually receives spironolactone after incomplete benefit from benzoyl peroxide and a retinoid. Which mechanism best explains why this systemic therapy is useful for her particular pattern?","options":["Blocking androgen-receptor signaling reduces hormonally driven sebaceous activity","Inhibiting bacterial 30S ribosomal translation reduces Cutibacterium acnes","Normalizing abnormal follicular desquamation through retinoid receptor activation","Occluding eccrine sweat ducts decreases sebum delivery to follicles"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: spironolactone acts as an antiandrogen and can reduce sebum-related acne activity.","Incorrect. This is the mechanism of tetracycline antibiotics, not spironolactone.","Incorrect. This is the principal retinoid action already being used.","Incorrect. Eccrine sweat ducts and sebaceous follicles are distinct; duct occlusion addresses hyperhidrosis."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q110","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A man with rosacea has fixed nasal enlargement, irregular contours and fibrotic thickening despite improvement of inflammatory papules after topical therapy. Which management objective best targets the residual anatomical pathology?","options":["Extend topical metronidazole indefinitely to dissolve fibrotic tissue","Procedurally reduce or remodel established hypertrophic nasal tissue","Increase oral doxycycline until fixed nasal volume normalizes","Treat presumed comedonal acne with topical tretinoin alone"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Anti-inflammatory topicals can control papules but do not reliably reverse established phymatous tissue.","Correct. Correct: surgical or ablative remodeling directly addresses fixed rhinophymatous enlargement.","Incorrect. Doxycycline treats inflammation but is not definitive tissue-volume reduction.","Incorrect. Comedonal acne is not the cause of fixed fibrotic rhinophyma."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q111","lecture":1,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A radiologist examines CT images of an adult trauma patient using slices that separate superior body regions from inferior regions. Each image displays structures at a particular vertical level rather than dividing anterior from posterior or right from left. Which anatomical plane was used?","options":["Sagittal plane","Coronal plane","Transverse (axial) plane","Median plane"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. This separates left and right portions.","Incorrect. This separates anterior and posterior portions.","Correct. An axial section separates superior from inferior portions of the body.","Incorrect. This is the sagittal plane through the exact midline."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q112","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A patient is evaluated following a shoulder dislocation. The examiner reviews how the glenohumeral articulation moves around multiple axes, while the elbow primarily undergoes flexion and extension. Which pairing of synovial joint types matches the shoulder and the principally uniaxial elbow articulation?","options":["Pivot; saddle","Condylar; plane","Hinge; ball-and-socket","Ball-and-socket; hinge"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. A pivot rotates about one longitudinal axis, and saddle geometry does not describe the shoulder.","Incorrect. Neither describes the multiaxial glenohumeral joint appropriately.","Incorrect. The classification order is reversed.","Correct. Glenohumeral articulation is multiaxial, while a typical hinge permits mainly flexion and extension."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q113","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"A patient recovering from a quadriceps injury slowly lowers the body while stepping down, and the knee flexes despite continued quadriceps activation. In a separate test, the same muscles produce tension against a fixed resistance while the knee angle remains constant. Which paired types of contraction describe the active muscle in these two tasks?","options":["Eccentric contraction during descent; isometric contraction against fixed resistance","Concentric during descent; isometric against fixed resistance","Isometric during descent; eccentric against fixed resistance","Eccentric during descent; concentric against fixed resistance"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. The active quadriceps lengthens to control knee flexion on descent and develops tension at fixed length against immovable resistance.","Incorrect. Concentric shortening does not explain active quadriceps lengthening during controlled lowering.","Incorrect. The first trial has joint motion and muscle length change; the second does not.","Incorrect. Concentric action requires shortening, absent in the immovable second trial."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q114","lecture":1,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 1","stem":"Following pelvic lymph-node surgery, a patient develops persistent unilateral leg swelling. Duplex ultrasonography confirms unobstructed deep venous flow, and there is no significant hypoalbuminemia. Interstitial proteins and fluid accumulate despite otherwise adequate vascular perfusion. Which consequence of lymphatic disruption best explains the edema?","options":["Increased arterial oxygen content raises interstitial oncotic pressure","Retained interstitial proteins favor persistent tissue fluid accumulation when lymphatic clearance is lost","An absence of venous valves directly blocks capillary filtration","Greater epidermal water evaporation causes deep tissue protein retention"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Arterial oxygen content is not the primary mechanism of lymphatic edema.","Correct. Lymphatics normally clear escaped proteins along with excess interstitial fluid; their loss sustains edema despite patent veins.","Incorrect. Patent venous drainage and the surgery-linked lymphatic disruption contradict this.","Incorrect. Surface evaporation does not account for impaired interstitial protein removal."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q115","lecture":2,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A student examines a spinal cord specimen with three protective meningeal layers. The deepest layer follows the contours of the spinal cord closely, unlike the more superficial layers separated by spaces. Which meninx directly invests the cord?","options":["Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater","Epidural fat"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Dura is the tough outer meningeal sleeve.","Incorrect. Arachnoid bridges over the subarachnoid space and is not closely adherent to the cord.","Correct. Pia closely adheres to the cord surface and helps form anchoring extensions.","Incorrect. Epidural fat is outside the dural sac and is not a meningeal layer."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q116","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"A patient develops cervical radicular pain after narrowing of the intervertebral foramen between C6 and C7. The clinician explains that cervical nerves generally exit above the vertebra with the same number, with an additional C8 spinal nerve at the cervicothoracic junction. Which nerve exits through the C6–C7 foramen?","options":["C6 spinal nerve","C8 spinal nerve","T1 spinal nerve","C7 spinal nerve"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. C6 exits at C5–C6.","Incorrect. C8 exits between C7 and T1.","Incorrect. T1 exits below T1 at T1–T2.","Correct. Cervical nerves C1–C7 exit above their numerically corresponding vertebrae; C7 exits at C6–C7."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q117","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"After vascular surgery, a patient experiences bilateral lower-extremity weakness and loss of pain and temperature below a thoracic level, yet vibration and joint-position sensation are relatively preserved. Imaging suggests ischemia of the anterior and lateral portions of the cord. Which pairing of vascular territory and tract locations accounts for the sensory sparing?","options":["Anterior spinal artery territory injures motor and anterolateral pathways while sparing posterior columns","Posterior spinal artery injury selectively spares dorsal columns","Dorsal root lesion interrupts bilateral motor tracts","Global ischemia injures only the posterior funiculi"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. The anterior two-thirds cord supply includes motor and spinothalamic pathways; the dorsal columns receive relatively independent posterior arterial supply.","Incorrect. Posterior arterial injury would tend to affect dorsal column function rather than preserve it.","Incorrect. A single root lesion is peripheral and does not explain bilateral tract-level dissociation.","Incorrect. Diffuse ischemia would not selectively preserve posterior columns in this pattern."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q118","lecture":2,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 2","stem":"Two patients have injuries confined to structures proximal to the formation of a mixed lumbar spinal nerve. The first loses voluntary motor function in muscles supplied by that segment but retains sensation; the second loses corresponding sensation without measurable muscle weakness. No spinal cord lesion is present. Which pairing of roots is affected in the two patients?","options":["Dorsal ramus first; ventral ramus second","Ventral root injury in the first patient; dorsal root injury in the second","Dorsal root first; ventral root second","Mixed spinal nerve first; dorsal ramus second"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Both rami are mixed, unlike the isolated deficits described.","Correct. Before union, ventral roots carry motor fibers and dorsal roots carry sensory fibers.","Incorrect. This reverses the functions of the roots.","Incorrect. Both contain sensory and motor fibers and do not fit selective pre-union injury."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q119","lecture":3,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A neurotransmitter binds a postsynaptic surface receptor and opens an ion channel before the membrane reaches the threshold for a voltage-gated action potential. Opening requires a chemical signaling molecule rather than direct mechanical membrane deformation or a change in transmembrane voltage. Which mechanism gates the channel?","options":["Voltage-gated channel","Mechanically gated channel","Ligand-gated channel","Constitutive leak channel"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Voltage-gated channels respond primarily to a change in transmembrane potential.","Incorrect. These respond to membrane deformation rather than neurotransmitter binding.","Correct. Binding of an extracellular neurotransmitter opens the receptor-associated channel.","Incorrect. Leak channels do not require discrete transmitter binding to open."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q120","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A presynaptic neuron fires several weak excitatory signals in rapid succession onto the same postsynaptic region. Although each depolarization alone is subthreshold, they overlap before dissipating and together trigger an action potential. Which type of synaptic summation is occurring?","options":["Spatial summation","Saltatory conduction","Absolute refractory period","Temporal summation"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Spatial summation combines inputs from different synaptic locations.","Incorrect. Saltation concerns impulse propagation along myelinated axons rather than synaptic integration.","Incorrect. The refractory period suppresses repeat action potentials and does not explain accumulation of graded inputs.","Correct. Rapid successive signals from the same terminal overlap before earlier graded potentials decay."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q121","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"A neuron maintains unchanged intracellular and extracellular sodium and potassium concentrations while a drug increases resting sodium-channel permeability. Its membrane potential shifts toward a more positive value, although the equilibrium potential for each ion is unchanged and ATP-dependent transport persists. Which principle determines the direction of the membrane-voltage shift?","options":["The membrane potential shifts toward the equilibrium potential of whichever ion becomes more permeant","Equilibrium potentials change solely because resting permeability changes","The sodium–potassium pump must reverse transport direction","The membrane remains fixed at the K equilibrium potential regardless of Na permeability"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. With constant gradients, increasing resting Na permeability weights membrane voltage more heavily toward the more positive Na equilibrium potential.","Incorrect. An ion equilibrium potential depends on concentration gradient and charge, not its membrane permeability.","Incorrect. No pump reversal is needed to explain the immediate permeability-driven shift.","Incorrect. Other permeant ions influence resting voltage when their relative conductance increases."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q122","lecture":3,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 3","stem":"An investigator delivers paired stimuli to a neuron. Immediately after a spike, the second stimulus fails regardless of its intensity; slightly later, a sufficiently strong stimulus triggers another spike while potassium conductance remains elevated. Which sequence of sodium-channel recovery and residual potassium-channel activity accounts for the difference?","options":["Initially closed but fully available Na channels prevent firing; later Na loss makes firing easier","Initially inactivated sodium channels prevent firing; later partial recovery with elevated K conductance raises threshold","Early K leakage alone makes any stimulus ineffective; later all Na channels remain inactivated","The Na/K ATPase ceases functioning then immediately restarts"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Closed available channels can activate and do not explain absolute refractoriness.","Correct. Absolute refractoriness reflects Na-channel inactivation, while relative refractoriness involves incomplete recovery and after-hyperpolarizing K currents.","Incorrect. Persistent complete Na inactivation would still prevent a second action potential.","Incorrect. Brief refractory phases principally arise from channel gating, not abrupt pump cycling."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q123","lecture":4,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A histologic tissue section shows cells each dominated by one large lipid droplet, leaving a thin peripheral rim of cytoplasm and a flattened peripheral nucleus. Lipid has dissolved during slide preparation, creating large clear spaces. Which adipose cell type is most consistent with this appearance?","options":["Brown multilocular adipocyte","Fibroblast","White (unilocular) adipocyte","Mast cell"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Brown cells contain multiple small lipid droplets and abundant mitochondria.","Incorrect. Fibroblasts are elongated ECM-producing cells rather than lipid-filled unilocular cells.","Correct. A single large lipid droplet displaces the nucleus peripherally, characteristic of white adipose tissue.","Incorrect. Mast cells contain mediator-rich granules, not one giant neutral-lipid droplet."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q124","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Cultured fibroblasts fail to adhere normally to a collagen-rich extracellular matrix after a membrane receptor family is experimentally blocked. The matrix proteins remain intact, but the linkage between cell cytoskeleton and extracellular adhesion proteins is disrupted. Which receptor family was inhibited?","options":["Desmogleins","Keratohyalin granules","Elastin","Integrins"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Desmogleins participate in intercellular desmosomal adhesion rather than primary cell–matrix anchoring.","Incorrect. These are involved in epidermal cornification, not fibroblast ECM receptor attachment.","Incorrect. Elastin is an extracellular structural protein, not a transmembrane receptor family.","Correct. Integrins are cell-surface adhesion receptors connecting matrix ligands to intracellular cytoskeletal structures."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q125","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"A tendon biopsy after injury shows loss of organized collagen bundles aligned with tensile loading, while a renal biopsy in a different patient shows disruption of a thin filtration-supporting basement membrane but relatively normal bulk interstitial fibers. Both are described as collagen defects. Which distinction between fibrillar and network-forming collagen architecture best predicts the respective mechanical consequences of the two injuries?","options":["Type I fibrillar collagen in tendon; type IV network-forming collagen in basement membrane","Type IV network in tendon; type I fibrils in basement membrane","Type II fibrils in tendon; type III network in basement membrane","Type VII fibrils in tendon; elastin network replacing collagen IV in kidney"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Type I assembles into aligned tensile fibrils; type IV forms a sheet-like basement membrane network supporting specialized interfaces.","Incorrect. This reverses their predominant tissue architecture.","Incorrect. Type II is prominent in cartilage and type III in reticular fibers; neither is the principal pairing here.","Incorrect. Type VII forms anchoring fibrils; elastin does not replace the type IV basement membrane collagen network."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q126","lecture":4,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 4","stem":"Six months after closure of a deep laceration, a patient has an intact scar without infection. The scar has become stronger over time as collagen fibers reorganize and cross-link, yet its mechanical behavior remains different from uninjured skin. Which property of scar remodeling explains progressive tensile-strength recovery without restoration of native architecture?","options":["The mature scar regains identical appendages and ECM architecture as normal skin","Collagen fibers progressively align and cross-link, improving strength without reconstructing normal skin architecture","Increasing leukocyte density alone directly provides the late tensile strength","Loss of all collagen cross-links increases scar tensile resistance"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Ordinary scars do not fully reproduce native specialized architecture.","Correct. Remodeling changes fibril organization and cross-links but scar differs structurally from uninjured skin.","Incorrect. Chronic inflammatory cell numbers do not substitute for collagen maturation.","Incorrect. Cross-linking generally contributes to strength rather than its absence."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q127","lecture":5,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A central nervous system white-matter specimen contains glial cells that extend processes around portions of multiple axons to produce compact myelin. These cells are distinct from the peripheral glia that wrap individual myelinated internodes. Which CNS glial cell is responsible?","options":["Schwann cell","Astrocyte","Oligodendrocyte","Microglial cell"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Schwann cells myelinate peripheral axons.","Incorrect. Astrocytes support CNS homeostasis rather than forming compact myelin.","Correct. Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons and can service multiple segments.","Incorrect. Microglia are CNS immune/phagocytic cells."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q128","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"Two ganglion specimens are compared. One contains closely packed pseudounipolar neurons surrounded by complete satellite-cell cuffs and lacks interneuronal synapses. The other contains scattered multipolar neurons and functional synapses with less complete satellite coverage. Which classification corresponds to each specimen?","options":["Autonomic ganglion first; dorsal root ganglion second","CNS nucleus first; dorsal root ganglion second","Dorsal root ganglion first; cerebral cortical gray matter second","Dorsal root ganglion first; autonomic ganglion second"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. The morphological and synaptic characteristics are reversed.","Incorrect. The first structure is a peripheral sensory ganglion, not a CNS nucleus.","Incorrect. The second is a peripheral autonomic ganglion with satellite cells, not cortical tissue.","Correct. DRG has pseudounipolar neurons with satellite cuffs and no local synapses; autonomic ganglia have multipolar neurons and synapses."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q129","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"After complete transection of a peripheral nerve, tissue distal to the injury develops axonal fragmentation. In affected neuronal somata, Nissl substance becomes dispersed during the injury response. Researchers selectively inhibit transport of signals and organelles from the distal axon toward the soma while preserving delivery of newly synthesized materials from the soma to the terminal. Which transport mechanism is selectively interrupted, and which observed pathological change is occurring in a different cellular compartment rather than representing failure of the transport motor itself?","options":["Dynein-mediated retrograde transport is blocked; Nissl dispersion is chromatolysis in the soma","Kinesin-mediated anterograde transport blocked; Wallerian degeneration occurs in soma","Dynein-mediated anterograde transport blocked; myelin fragmentation is chromatolysis","Kinesin-mediated retrograde transport blocked; dispersion of Nissl is nodal demyelination"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Dynein carries cargo toward the soma; chromatolysis describes a separate somatic response to axonal injury.","Incorrect. Kinesin generally moves outward, and Wallerian degeneration involves the distal axon.","Incorrect. Direction is wrong and chromatolysis describes somatic Nissl changes.","Incorrect. Kinesin is typically anterograde, and Nissl dispersion is not nodal myelin loss."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q130","lecture":5,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 5","stem":"A patient exposed to a neurotoxic medication develops peripheral neuropathy with marked loss of functioning axons on biopsy. Another has severe conduction slowing with segmental myelin loss despite preservation of most axons. Which pairing of primary structural injury and resulting conduction defect distinguishes these processes?","options":["Axonal degeneration primarily increases conduction speed; demyelination raises functional fiber number","Axonal degeneration primarily reduces functioning fiber number; segmental demyelination primarily slows or blocks conduction in surviving axons","Both disorders consist only of receptor failure at neuromuscular junctions","Axonal injury preserves all distal fibers while demyelination necessarily transects axons"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Neither consequence matches structural loss.","Correct. Loss of axonal continuity reduces conducting fibers, whereas loss of insulation disrupts saltatory transmission on relatively intact axons.","Incorrect. The pathology lies within nerve fibers, not isolated postsynaptic receptors.","Incorrect. Axonal injury destroys distal continuity; demyelination can initially spare axons."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q131","lecture":6,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"An anatomical specimen from near the abdominal aorta contains sympathetic ganglion cells that receive fibers passing through the paravertebral chain without synapsing. This location is neither inside the target organ nor directly in the chain beside the vertebral column. How is the ganglion classified?","options":["Paravertebral ganglion","Dorsal root ganglion","Prevertebral (collateral) ganglion","Intrinsic ganglion"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Paravertebral ganglia form the longitudinal sympathetic chain beside vertebrae.","Incorrect. DRG contains primary sensory somata, not an autonomic motor synapse.","Correct. Sympathetic ganglia near the abdominal aorta, reached by splanchnic preganglionic fibers, are prevertebral.","Incorrect. Intrinsic ganglia lie in or very near target-organ walls."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q132","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A woman develops reduced pelvic organ parasympathetic function after surgery affecting nerves emerging from sacral cord levels S2–S4. The affected fibers enter autonomic plexuses in the pelvis without entering the sympathetic chain. Which named nerves are damaged?","options":["Lumbar splanchnic nerves","White rami communicantes","Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves","Pelvic splanchnic nerves"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Lumbar splanchnics are sympathetic routes associated with prevertebral ganglia.","Incorrect. White rami carry sympathetic fibers from thoracolumbar levels to the sympathetic chain.","Incorrect. Greater thoracic splanchnics carry sympathetic fibers toward abdominal ganglia.","Correct. These nerves carry sacral parasympathetic preganglionic fibers from S2–S4 into pelvic plexuses."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q133","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"A patient has a small unilateral patch of dry skin on the trunk after an operation near the sympathetic chain. Ordinary light-touch sensation and voluntary muscle strength are preserved. The lesion selectively interrupts postganglionic fibers traveling from the chain into the spinal nerve, but does not damage sensory afferents or somatic motor axons. Which route normally returns these sympathetic fibers to the spinal nerve, and what downstream effector explains the dry skin?","options":["Gray ramus communicans returns sympathetic postganglionic axons to spinal nerve; sudomotor fibers innervate eccrine glands","White ramus carries parasympathetic fibers to sebaceous glands","Dorsal root carries sweat-gland efferents into the spinal cord","Ventral root directly innervates sweat glands using somatic motor axons"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Gray rami exist at all levels; postganglionic cholinergic sympathetic signaling normally stimulates eccrine sweating.","Incorrect. White rami are thoracolumbar sympathetic preganglionic pathways, not parasympathetic cutaneous output.","Incorrect. Dorsal roots are sensory rather than peripheral autonomic efferent exits.","Incorrect. Somatic motor axons innervate skeletal muscle, not eccrine sweat glands."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q134","lecture":6,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 6","stem":"During a pelvic surgical procedure, clinicians discuss two organs on opposite sides of the pelvic pain line. Nociceptive afferents from the upper organ generally accompany sympathetic pathways toward thoracolumbar ganglia, whereas those from the lower organ follow sacral parasympathetic routes. Which pairing of afferent routes is correct?","options":["Above: parasympathetic to S2–S4; below: sympathetic to T1–L2","Above the pelvic pain line: sympathetic routes to T1–L2 DRG; below: parasympathetic routes to S2–S4 DRG","Both follow somatic motor fibers to anterior horn cells","Both travel only by cranial vagal sensory pathways"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. The typical visceral pain routes are reversed.","Correct. Pain afferents generally track sympathetics above the line and parasympathetics below it, with different DRG destinations.","Incorrect. Visceral pain is afferent and enters sensory DRG pathways, not somatic motor horn cells.","Incorrect. Pelvic nociceptive afferents do not exclusively route through the vagus."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q135","lecture":7,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"Cervical spine imaging shows a joint where the dens of the axis is held against the ring of the atlas. Its predominant normal movement is side-to-side rotation, as when a person shakes the head to indicate no. Which synovial joint type best fits this arrangement?","options":["Hinge joint","Ball-and-socket joint","Pivot synovial joint","Plane gliding joint"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Hinge joints chiefly flex and extend, not pivot about a dens.","Incorrect. Ball-and-socket joints allow multiaxial rotation around a rounded head.","Correct. The median atlantoaxial articulation enables rotation around the dens.","Incorrect. Plane joints permit sliding, not the characteristic median dens pivot."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q136","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"Following trauma near the medial border of the scapula, a patient has difficulty retracting the shoulder blade. Examination isolates weakness of rhomboid muscles, while shoulder abduction and elbow flexion remain normal. Which nerve is most likely affected?","options":["Thoracodorsal nerve","Spinal accessory nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Thoracodorsal innervates latissimus dorsi.","Incorrect. CN XI provides motor innervation to trapezius.","Incorrect. Long thoracic innervates serratus anterior, important for scapular protraction and stability.","Correct. Rhomboids retract the scapula and are principally supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q137","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"A gymnast has an L5 pars defect and normal vertebral alignment. Six months later, the same patient develops forward translation of L5 relative to S1 without a new fracture elsewhere. A second patient has an identical pars defect but no displacement after stopping repetitive hyperextension training. Which change in vertebral mechanics must have occurred in the first case to account for the new alignment abnormality, and which imaging finding would distinguish it from an isolated pars defect?","options":["Isolated spondylolysis persists in both patients because a pars defect excludes vertebral translation.","The first patient develops degenerative spondylosis, identified by any increase in pain without a change in alignment.","The first patient develops isthmic spondylolisthesis, documented by anterior translation of L5 on S1; the second has isolated spondylolysis.","Both patients develop spinal stenosis, because the pars defect always narrows the canal even when alignment is preserved."],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. A pars defect can coexist with anterior translation; the latter is spondylolisthesis.","Incorrect. Pain alone and degenerative changes do not define the observed new vertebral translation.","Correct. Both have a pars defect, but only the first demonstrates displacement.","Incorrect. A pars defect does not necessarily produce central canal stenosis."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q138","lecture":7,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 7","stem":"Two motor vehicle collision patients have cervical injuries with no vertebral fractures. One experiences abrupt hyperextension with separation of anterior vertebral-body margins; another experiences excessive flexion with damage to posterior midline ligamentous structures. Both retain an intact vertebral canal. Which pairing of injured ligaments with their normally restrained movements best differentiates the two mechanical failure patterns?","options":["Anterior longitudinal ligament injury with hyperextension; posterior midline interspinous/supraspinous injury with hyperflexion.","Posterior longitudinal ligament injury with hyperextension; anterior longitudinal ligament injury with flexion.","Ligamentum flavum injury with hyperextension; annulus fibrosus injury with flexion.","Anterior longitudinal ligament injury with flexion; nuchal ligament injury with hyperextension."],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. ALL resists hyperextension; posterior interspinous and supraspinous ligaments resist flexion.","Incorrect. This reverses the key restraint associated with anterior and posterior ligament positions.","Incorrect. Ligamentum flavum lies between laminae and is not the anterior vertebral body ligament implicated.","Incorrect. The ALL is an important restraint against hyperextension, while posterior cervical midline structures resist flexion."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q139","lecture":8,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"In an epidermal biopsy, a sparse population of cells in the basal layer sends processes among neighboring keratinocytes. The cells synthesize pigment and transfer pigment-containing structures to surrounding keratinocytes, contributing to skin coloration and UV protection. Which cell type is described?","options":["Merkel cell","Langerhans cell","Melanocyte","Fibroblast"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Merkel cells are sensory epithelial cells associated with light touch.","Incorrect. Langerhans cells primarily participate in antigen presentation.","Correct. Melanocytes synthesize melanin and transfer pigment-containing granules to neighboring keratinocytes.","Incorrect. Fibroblasts synthesize connective tissue matrix rather than epidermal pigment."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q140","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A histology specimen demonstrates a coiled dermal sweat gland with secretory cells that release material while retaining their cytoplasm and remaining viable. Ductal cells subsequently modify the ionic content of the secretion. Which general secretory mechanism is used by the eccrine gland?","options":["Holocrine secretion","Apocrine decapitation","Endocrine secretion","Merocrine secretion"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Holocrine release involves destruction of the entire secreting cell, typical of sebaceous glands.","Incorrect. Apocrine release uses apical cytoplasm and is not the described eccrine mechanism.","Incorrect. Endocrine refers to hormone delivery into circulation rather than sweat release to the surface.","Correct. Secretory products are released without loss of the whole eccrine gland cell."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q141","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A man suffers deep dermal and subcutaneous injury and subsequently loses much of his ability to detect deep pressure and vibration, while superficial light-touch discrimination is relatively preserved. The damaged receptor is normally encapsulated and located deeper than papillary dermal touch receptors. Which receptor and location best explain his deficit?","options":["Pacinian corpuscle in deep dermis or hypodermis","Meissner corpuscle in papillary dermis","Merkel cell in stratum basale","Free nerve ending in superficial epidermis"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Pacinian receptors lie deep and detect vibration and deep pressure; Meissner receptors provide more superficial fine touch.","Incorrect. Meissner corpuscles are superficial light-touch receptors.","Incorrect. Merkel cells detect light touch and are situated in basal epidermis.","Incorrect. Free endings transmit pain and thermal information rather than specialized deep vibration."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q142","lecture":8,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 8","stem":"A patient with generalized hypopigmentation since infancy has an intact basal melanocyte population and normal keratinocyte UV-response signaling, but little pigment reaches surrounding keratinocytes. A second patient has sharply defined acquired white patches associated with autoimmune disease, and affected skin contains few melanocytes. Which distinct lesion in the UV-to-pigment pathway explains the contrasting biopsy findings, rather than attributing both to reduced UV exposure?","options":["Both involve primary loss of melanocytes from birth","Congenital pigment synthesis defect despite preserved melanocytes; acquired autoimmune melanocyte loss in depigmented patches","Both involve only UV avoidance with otherwise intact pigment biology","First involves dermal collagen loss; second eccrine duct failure"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. The first biopsy retains melanocytes and the second is acquired.","Correct. Albinism-like disorders retain melanocytes with impaired melanin synthesis/transfer; vitiligo destroys melanocytes in affected areas.","Incorrect. The patients have distinct cellular lesions, not simply decreased sunlight.","Incorrect. Neither mechanism principally governs melanocyte number or pigment synthesis."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q143","lecture":9,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A patient has a flat, sharply demarcated 3-cm area of changed pigmentation on the forearm. The skin surface is otherwise intact, and the lesion is neither elevated nor depressed. Which term best describes this lesion on the basis of its size and lack of elevation?","options":["Macule","Plaque","Patch","Papule"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. A macule is similarly flat but smaller than the described 3-cm area.","Incorrect. A plaque is a palpable raised plateau-like lesion.","Correct. A flat, nonpalpable discoloration greater than 1 cm is a patch.","Incorrect. A papule is a small raised solid lesion."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q144","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A patient with tense blisters undergoes biopsy revealing subepidermal separation. A second specimen is taken from uninvolved-appearing skin next to a fresh blister to demonstrate immunoglobulin already deposited along the dermal–epidermal junction. Which immunofluorescence test is most appropriate for this tissue-bound material?","options":["Indirect immunofluorescence on serum and donor substrate","ELISA on serum for antigen-specific antibody levels","Routine H&E histology alone","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. IIF primarily evaluates circulating antibodies, not tissue-bound deposits.","Incorrect. ELISA characterizes circulating antigen-specific antibodies, not their tissue deposition pattern.","Incorrect. H&E can show split location but not an antibody deposition pattern by fluorescence.","Correct. DIF of perilesional skin shows deposited antibodies in the patient’s tissue before erosion destroys the pattern."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q145","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A woman develops painful mucosal erosions and fragile blisters with histologic separation among epidermal keratinocytes. An older man develops tense pruritic blisters with separation beneath the entire epidermis. Which pairing of impaired adhesion structures accounts for these differences in blister depth and roof strength?","options":["Desmosomal cell–cell adhesion first; hemidesmosomal basal cell–matrix adhesion second","Hemidesmosomes first; desmosomes second","Type I collagen fibrils first; type IV collagen network second","Melanocyte–keratinocyte transfer first; sebaceous gland secretion second"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Intraepidermal acantholysis causes fragile lesions; subepidermal separation preserves a full epidermal roof and yields tense bullae.","Incorrect. This reverses the adhesion structures and blister planes.","Incorrect. These do not directly describe the immune adhesion failures given.","Incorrect. Pigment transfer and sebum do not cause the specified blister cleavage planes."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q146","lecture":9,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 9","stem":"A man with gluten-sensitive enteropathy develops intensely itchy extensor vesicles. Biopsy shows dermal papillary neutrophilic microabscesses and granular IgA deposition. Dapsone rapidly improves the skin symptoms even before strict dietary adherence. What mechanism most directly explains this early improvement?","options":["Removal of IgA antibodies from the intestinal circulation","Reduced neutrophil activity in the dermal papillae","Depletion of CD20-positive B lymphocytes","Direct inhibition of melanocyte melanin synthesis"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Dapsone does not remove the gluten-related immune trigger or directly clear all IgA deposits.","Correct. Correct: dapsone reduces neutrophil-associated inflammation in dermatitis herpetiformis.","Incorrect. Anti-CD20 therapy describes rituximab, not dapsone.","Incorrect. Melanin synthesis is unrelated to the neutrophil-rich blistering process."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q147","lecture":10,"difficulty":"In-House","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A student reviews hair-follicle phases. During one phase, germinal matrix cells proliferate and actively generate the hair shaft, before the follicle enters a shorter regression phase and then a resting phase. Which phase corresponds to active hair production?","options":["Catagen","Telogen","Anagen","Exogen"],"answer":"C","expl":["Incorrect. Catagen is the transitional regression phase.","Incorrect. Telogen is the resting stage before shedding.","Correct. Anagen is the actively growing hair-follicle phase with matrix proliferation.","Incorrect. Exogen refers to hair-shaft release and is not the primary active-growth phase."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q148","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Intermediate","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A woman presents with persistent central facial erythema and inflammatory papules triggered by sun exposure; no comedones are identified. A dermatologist selects topical ivermectin for papulopustular rosacea rather than treating presumed acne. Which possible pathogenic contributor is specifically targeted by ivermectin?","options":["Androgen-dependent sebum secretion","Dermatophyte invasion of hair shafts","Permanent hypertrophic nasal tissue","Demodex mites"],"answer":"D","expl":["Incorrect. Antiandrogen therapy such as spironolactone targets this acne mechanism.","Incorrect. Tinea capitis requires systemic antifungals, not rosacea ivermectin.","Incorrect. Established phymatous thickening typically requires tissue-directed procedural treatment.","Correct. Topical ivermectin can address a Demodex-associated component of papulopustular rosacea."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q149","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A patient with focal palmar hyperhidrosis has failed topical aluminum chloride. The clinician contrasts oral glycopyrrolate with intradermal botulinum toxin. Which paired action correctly identifies where each therapy interrupts cholinergic stimulation of eccrine glands?","options":["Glycopyrrolate blocks glandular muscarinic receptors; botulinum toxin reduces presynaptic acetylcholine release","Glycopyrrolate blocks nicotinic receptors at sympathetic ganglia; botulinum toxin increases ACh breakdown in the duct","Glycopyrrolate inhibits choline acetyltransferase; botulinum toxin competitively antagonizes muscarinic receptors","Both treatments obstruct the eccrine sweat duct by precipitating intraluminal salts"],"answer":"A","expl":["Correct. Correct: one acts postsynaptically at muscarinic receptors and the other inhibits nerve-terminal ACh release.","Incorrect. Glycopyrrolate is antimuscarinic; botulinum toxin does not work by intraductal acetylcholinesterase activation.","Incorrect. Neither is best described by those mechanisms; they target receptor signaling and presynaptic vesicle release, respectively.","Incorrect. Duct obstruction is the approach of aluminum chloride, not these alternatives."]},{"id":"msk_w13_ca_q150","lecture":10,"difficulty":"Step 1","topic":"Lecture 10","stem":"A woman has diffuse scalp shedding three months after a major operation; her hair follicles remain present and her scalp is noninflamed. Another woman develops years of progressive widening of the central hair part with finer terminal hairs. Which paired follicular mechanism explains the contrast in time course and distribution?","options":["Irreversible follicular destruction in both women","Delayed shift of many follicles into telogen versus gradual androgen-associated follicular miniaturization","Follicular dermatophyte infection versus autoimmune attack causing smooth focal patches","Progressive miniaturization after surgery versus synchronized telogen shift in patterned loss"],"answer":"B","expl":["Incorrect. Both are typically nonscarring; follicles remain present.","Correct. Correct: telogen effluvium reflects a post-stressor hair-cycle shift, while androgenetic alopecia causes pattern-associated miniaturization.","Incorrect. Neither course matches the described noninfectious diffuse and patterned presentations.","Incorrect. This reverses the characteristic patterns and time courses."]}];
